In my underwear.

763
00:28:48,190 --> 00:28:50,690
I'm speaking
for the pilots and some
of the Air Force boys.

764
00:28:50,690 --> 00:28:52,700
For the laughter, for
the love, for the boys.

765
00:28:52,690 --> 00:28:54,190
We propose launching
a rescue craft

766
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:56,200
using one of their
boosters.

767
00:28:56,200 --> 00:28:59,200
He is plucky.
He is.

768
00:28:59,200 --> 00:29:00,700
Dougherty, you haven't
been listening.

769
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:02,710
Courtney, will you repeat
that trend data

770
00:29:02,700 --> 00:29:05,200
on oxygen consumption
in the spacecraft?

771
00:29:05,210 --> 00:29:08,710
As of now,
they're going to live
another 42 hours,

772
00:29:08,710 --> 00:29:10,210
plus or minus 2%.

773
00:29:10,210 --> 00:29:11,710
Oh, man,
am I hung over.

774
00:29:11,710 --> 00:29:13,220
Now, that takes us up
to 10:30 Monday night.

775
00:29:13,210 --> 00:29:16,710
There's a launch
window for a shot
at 8:48 Monday night.

776
00:29:16,720 --> 00:29:19,220
I don't wanna. No.

777
00:29:19,720 --> 00:29:20,720
An Air Force Titan
3C corp

778
00:29:20,720 --> 00:29:23,230
left Denver for the Cape
one hour ago.

779
00:29:23,220 --> 00:29:24,720
Oh, actually, it was
already in the pipe.

780
00:29:24,730 --> 00:29:27,730
And also the XRV lifting
body from Edwards.

781
00:29:27,730 --> 00:29:29,730
Can you get
the Titan and an XRV

782
00:29:30,230 --> 00:29:32,240
on the pad, ready to go,
in 42 hours?

783
00:29:32,230 --> 00:29:34,230
Some of our boys
think they could.

784
00:29:34,230 --> 00:29:35,730
A non-man-rated booster

785
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:37,240
and a craft
that's never flown.

786
00:29:37,740 --> 00:29:39,240
Oh, it'll fly all right,

787
00:29:39,240 --> 00:29:41,240
and Dougherty here has
all the qualifications.

788
00:29:41,740 --> 00:29:43,750
No, no, no, no.

789
00:29:43,740 --> 00:29:47,740
Proposal is rejected.
Anything else?

790
00:29:47,750 --> 00:29:49,250
Well, we could have
a fundraising carnival.

791
00:29:49,750 --> 00:29:51,750
Yeah, what do I tell
the pilots?

792
00:29:51,750 --> 00:29:53,260
Tell them that the request

793
00:29:53,250 --> 00:29:55,750
is outside the area
of their competence.

794
00:29:55,760 --> 00:29:56,760
Oh, you mean
they're too dumb?

795
00:29:56,760 --> 00:29:58,260
We've got three men
up there dying.

796
00:29:58,260 --> 00:30:00,260
They're professionals.
If they were here now,

797
00:30:00,260 --> 00:30:02,270
they'd say take
what you've learned

798
00:30:02,260 --> 00:30:03,260
and get on with
the next mission.

799
00:30:03,260 --> 00:30:05,760
I don't give a damn
about the next mission!

800
00:30:05,770 --> 00:30:07,270
Dougherty, there's no room
in this program

801
00:30:07,270 --> 00:30:08,770
for a chief astronaut

802
00:30:08,770 --> 00:30:10,270
who cannot look
at the whole picture.

803
00:30:10,270 --> 00:30:12,780
Then fire me.

804
00:30:12,770 --> 00:30:14,770
Sober up and then
we'll talk about it.

805
00:30:14,780 --> 00:30:16,780
You want to launch
a rescue mission

806
00:30:16,780 --> 00:30:18,780
in 42 hours. All right.

807
00:30:18,780 --> 00:30:20,780
So they can't
get back to Earth.

808
00:30:20,780 --> 00:30:22,290
Is that
what they're saying?

809
00:30:22,280 --> 00:30:25,780
Stage one, stage two
and trans stage.

810
00:30:25,790 --> 00:30:28,290
How long, Colonel,
on past performance?

811
00:30:28,290 --> 00:30:30,790
On past performance,
12 days, but if we were--

812
00:30:30,790 --> 00:30:32,300
12 days.

813
00:30:32,290 --> 00:30:36,790
Two, build up and strap on
solid fuel stacks.

814
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:38,300
Four days, but we can--

815
00:30:38,300 --> 00:30:39,800
- Four days.
- Third base.

816
00:30:39,800 --> 00:30:41,300
Check out launch weather
for Monday night.

817
00:30:41,300 --> 00:30:42,810
There's a hurricane
out there.

818
00:30:42,800 --> 00:30:45,300
600 miles off the coast
and headed out to sea.

819
00:30:45,310 --> 00:30:46,310
Well, you're in luck.

820
00:30:46,310 --> 00:30:48,810
Three, make the vehicle
to the bird

821
00:30:48,810 --> 00:30:50,810
and check out configuration.
How long, Colonel?

822
00:30:50,810 --> 00:30:54,320
Duh, I left my notes
at home.

823
00:30:54,310 --> 00:30:56,310
Sorry, so much.

824
00:30:56,320 --> 00:30:57,320
Couldn't do it
under five days.

825
00:30:57,320 --> 00:30:59,820
Four, compute navigation
and guidance tapes

826
00:30:59,820 --> 00:31:01,820
for first
apogee rendezvous.

827
00:31:01,820 --> 00:31:04,330
10 days.
Program computers.

828
00:31:04,320 --> 00:31:05,820
So what's
your point?

829
00:31:05,830 --> 00:31:07,330
Three weeks.

830
00:31:07,330 --> 00:31:08,830
We'll go without a computer.
We'll go without a checkup.

831
00:31:09,330 --> 00:31:11,330
And crew training
in a craft

832
00:31:11,330 --> 00:31:12,340
which has not been
man-rated,

833
00:31:12,330 --> 00:31:14,830
which cannot be flown
by one pilot

834
00:31:14,840 --> 00:31:17,340
and which has not
been modified
to hold four men.

835
00:31:17,340 --> 00:31:18,340
Hey, wake up.

836
00:31:18,340 --> 00:31:19,340
How about that one?

837
00:31:19,340 --> 00:31:20,840
Scorched you, huh?
Face.

838
00:31:20,840 --> 00:31:22,350
We'll do it in 42 hours.

839
00:31:22,340 --> 00:31:24,340
Or my name isn't
Harry O., U.S. Treasury!

840
00:31:24,340 --> 00:31:28,340
While you're assembling
your rescue ship in 42 hours

841
00:31:28,350 --> 00:31:30,850
and spending--well, how much
would that cost, Fisher?

842
00:31:30,850 --> 00:31:31,850
50 million.

843
00:31:31,850 --> 00:31:33,860
- So what?
- Plus how many deaths?

844
00:31:33,850 --> 00:31:37,350
Three, sir. Maybe.

845
00:31:37,360 --> 00:31:40,360
In a program
of this magnitude,

846
00:31:40,360 --> 00:31:43,870
in which all nominal safety
factors are disregarded,

847
00:31:43,860 --> 00:31:46,360
I calculate .02%
of the workforce will--

848
00:31:46,870 --> 00:31:49,370
Just how in the hell
do you know how many
people are gonna die?

849
00:31:49,370 --> 00:31:51,870
You think we haven't
considered rescue?
Here it is.

850
00:31:51,870 --> 00:31:53,380
Rescue and every other
contingency.

851
00:31:53,370 --> 00:31:54,370
Here it is.

852
00:31:54,370 --> 00:31:56,370
Reduced to digitals
and computerized.

853
00:31:56,380 --> 00:31:57,380
There's Bill Shatner's
report, right here.

854
00:31:57,380 --> 00:31:59,380
You helped to write it.

855
00:31:59,380 --> 00:32:01,880
A rational approach.

856
00:32:01,880 --> 00:32:03,390
Well, screw your
rational approach.

857
00:32:03,380 --> 00:32:04,380
Yeah, well, bite me,
Harry O.

858
00:32:04,380 --> 00:32:05,880
Three of your pilots
are in trouble,

859
00:32:05,890 --> 00:32:07,890
and if you don't
try to get them down,
well, goddamn you!

860
00:32:07,890 --> 00:32:08,890
Whoa!

861
00:32:08,890 --> 00:32:11,390
You got me.
That was fair.

862
00:32:11,390 --> 00:32:12,900
Well, you have feelings.
Good. I have feelings.

863
00:32:12,890 --> 00:32:15,890
That's why
we live by the rules.

864
00:32:15,900 --> 00:32:17,900
Ape law.

865
00:32:17,900 --> 00:32:18,900
- Wheeler.
- Woolsey.

866
00:32:18,900 --> 00:32:22,910
- Courtney. Fisher.
- Price.

867
00:32:22,900 --> 00:32:24,900
- Klein. Degrote.
- Calvin.

868
00:32:24,910 --> 00:32:26,410
We've got a bingo.

869
00:32:26,410 --> 00:32:27,910
Those men up there
are your friends.

870
00:32:27,910 --> 00:32:31,410
Can you launch a rescue
mission in 42 hours?

871
00:32:31,410 --> 00:32:33,920
You bet we can.
We'll get a barn
and I'll paint the sets.

872
00:32:33,910 --> 00:32:36,910
We'll put on a rescue mission
that'll take it us
straight to Broadway!

873
00:32:36,920 --> 00:32:38,420
( buzzer )

874
00:32:38,420 --> 00:32:39,920
Time's up.
How much did you risk?

875
00:32:39,920 --> 00:32:41,920
All of it? I'm sorry.

876
00:32:41,920 --> 00:32:43,930
It's Commissioner Gordon.

877
00:32:43,920 --> 00:32:45,420
Yeah, we're ready
at this end.

878
00:32:48,430 --> 00:32:49,430
- Uh-huh.
- Very good.

879
00:32:49,430 --> 00:32:50,430
The girls are here.

880
00:32:50,930 --> 00:32:54,940
The President
will take the call
at 7:00 his time.

881
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:02,150
Ironman One,
this is Houston Flight.

882
00:33:02,140 --> 00:33:03,640
Go, Houston.

883
00:33:04,140 --> 00:33:06,140
We're coming up
to get you, Ironman.

884
00:33:06,150 --> 00:33:08,150
- We've called AAA.
- Say again?

885
00:33:08,150 --> 00:33:10,150
We're coming up
to get you.

886
00:33:10,150 --> 00:33:11,150
That's what
I thought he said.

887
00:33:11,150 --> 00:33:12,160
We're launching a rescue
mission from the Cape.

888
00:33:12,150 --> 00:33:13,650
Hey, it's helmet day
at NASA.

889
00:33:13,650 --> 00:33:15,650
David Janssen wishes
it was beer day.

890
00:33:15,660 --> 00:33:17,660
Modified to hold four men.

891
00:33:17,660 --> 00:33:19,660
Now, there are men
without hats.

892
00:33:19,660 --> 00:33:20,660
Oh.

893
00:33:20,660 --> 00:33:23,670
Do you read, Ironman?

894
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:25,660
Yeah, we read you.

895
00:33:25,670 --> 00:33:26,670
Yeah, whatever.

896
00:33:26,670 --> 00:33:28,670
Houston, when do you
estimate launch?

897
00:33:28,670 --> 00:33:31,170
Just over 40 hours,
Stoney.

898
00:33:31,170 --> 00:33:35,180
40 hours?
Who are they kidding?

899
00:33:37,680 --> 00:33:39,180
Do you read, Ironman?

900
00:33:39,180 --> 00:33:41,680
No, but I read "Final Exit,"
and it's gonna come in handy.

901
00:33:41,680 --> 00:33:43,190
We've decided to make
a move up here.

902
00:33:43,180 --> 00:33:45,180
Go EVA and eyeball
the engines.

903
00:33:45,190 --> 00:33:47,190
The fuel valves
could be frozen.

904
00:33:47,190 --> 00:33:48,690
We'll pull
the insulating hatches.

905
00:33:48,690 --> 00:33:50,190
If they are,
when we get in the sun,

906
00:33:50,190 --> 00:33:52,700
we'll try and get
some heat on 'em.

907
00:33:52,690 --> 00:33:55,190
That's an interesting
thought, Ironman,

908
00:33:55,200 --> 00:33:57,200
but we have no indication
of that here

909
00:33:57,200 --> 00:33:59,200
or anything else
you could correct.

910
00:33:59,200 --> 00:34:02,210
We're all agreed
the rescue mission is it.

911
00:34:02,200 --> 00:34:03,200
Well, we're
all agreed here

912
00:34:03,200 --> 00:34:04,700
that we have to take
some affirmative action

913
00:34:04,710 --> 00:34:06,210
to repair our vehicle.

914
00:34:06,210 --> 00:34:07,210
Well, we're gonna hire
some women and minorities.

915
00:34:07,210 --> 00:34:08,210
Ironman, standby one.

916
00:34:08,210 --> 00:34:11,210
Tell them negative,
Jimmy, negative.

917
00:34:11,210 --> 00:34:12,220
Always with
the negative waves.

918
00:34:12,710 --> 00:34:15,710
Jim, we appreciate
your desire to take
affirmative action

919
00:34:15,720 --> 00:34:17,720
and we need your
affirmative action

920
00:34:17,720 --> 00:34:19,220
if the mission
is to succeed.

921
00:34:19,220 --> 00:34:20,220
But shut up!

922
00:34:20,220 --> 00:34:23,230
The most critical factor
is your oxygen supply.

923
00:34:23,220 --> 00:34:27,220
You get 15 minutes
of activity outside
the spacecraft

924
00:34:27,230 --> 00:34:28,230
to eyeball the engine,

925
00:34:28,230 --> 00:34:29,730
you're going to have
to trade off

926
00:34:29,730 --> 00:34:31,730
three or four hours
of passive breathing.

927
00:34:31,730 --> 00:34:33,240
Hmm.

928
00:34:33,230 --> 00:34:35,230
From where we sit,
you don't have
that oxygen to spare.

929
00:34:35,240 --> 00:34:37,240
Well, what's your Malibu
Barbie watch say there?

930
00:34:37,240 --> 00:34:38,240
Therefore,
we'd like to suggest

931
00:34:38,240 --> 00:34:41,240
that you go
into low-tide mode,

932
00:34:41,240 --> 00:34:46,250
lower your
oxygen pressure to 3.5,

933
00:34:46,250 --> 00:34:49,250
execute a full
emergency power down

934
00:34:49,250 --> 00:34:50,250
and take your pills.

935
00:34:50,250 --> 00:34:51,750
Hey!

936
00:34:51,750 --> 00:34:53,260
They want us to buy it
while we're sleeping.

937
00:34:53,750 --> 00:34:55,250
We got Judy Garland
on the line.

938
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:57,260
She'll be advising you
on the pill thing.

939
00:34:57,260 --> 00:34:59,260
Do you copy, Ironman?

940
00:34:59,260 --> 00:35:01,260
- What? Oh.
- Yeah.

941
00:35:01,260 --> 00:35:03,270
We're thinking about it.

942
00:35:03,260 --> 00:35:04,260
Not!

943
00:35:04,260 --> 00:35:06,260
Tell them negative, Jim,
negative.

944
00:35:09,770 --> 00:35:11,770
- Pruett.
- Is this thing on?

945
00:35:11,770 --> 00:35:12,780
Now this is Keith.

946
00:35:12,770 --> 00:35:14,270
I'm down here at
the Cape with Sadler.

947
00:35:14,270 --> 00:35:15,770
I'm doing karaoke.

948
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:17,780
And we're getting this
rescue shot ready to go.

949
00:35:17,780 --> 00:35:19,280
Now, don't do
anything foolish.

950
00:35:19,280 --> 00:35:21,780
Don't climb into
your hard suits.

951
00:35:21,780 --> 00:35:24,290
Don't get out your tools and
don't try to fix the engine.

952
00:35:24,280 --> 00:35:25,780
Don't sleep in
the subways, baby.

953
00:35:25,790 --> 00:35:27,290
Just take your pills
and go to sleep.

954
00:35:27,290 --> 00:35:28,790
And don't eat
refrozen ice cream

955
00:35:28,790 --> 00:35:30,790
and don't mix
household cleaners.

956
00:35:30,790 --> 00:35:32,800
We've got a better chance
than you do, Jim.

957
00:35:32,790 --> 00:35:34,790
- ( all laugh )
- We'll be back at you.

958
00:35:36,800 --> 00:35:39,800
Buzz, you eat up
25% more oxygen

959
00:35:39,800 --> 00:35:41,300
every time
you lose your cool.

960
00:35:41,300 --> 00:35:44,310
What's a four letter word
for fiery death?

961
00:35:46,310 --> 00:35:47,810
Houston, Ironman.

962
00:35:47,810 --> 00:35:49,810
Please pull ahead
to the first window.

963
00:35:49,810 --> 00:35:51,810
- Preparing for power down.
- Roger, Ironman.

964
00:35:51,810 --> 00:35:53,820
Hmm.

965
00:35:53,810 --> 00:35:54,810
What are the numbers,
Stoney?

966
00:35:55,320 --> 00:35:58,320
Cleveland 8,
Cincinnati 4. Uh-oh.

967
00:35:58,320 --> 00:36:00,320
Well, with nominal
oxygen usage,

968
00:36:00,320 --> 00:36:01,320
that'll make it
36 hours left.

969
00:36:01,320 --> 00:36:02,330
Uh-huh.

970
00:36:02,820 --> 00:36:06,320
Take out the pad,
40 hours, more or less.

971
00:36:06,330 --> 00:36:08,830
I've got sleep apnea,
so I won't use up much.

972
00:36:08,830 --> 00:36:11,830
How you figuring
the residual amounts
in the tanks?

973
00:36:11,830 --> 00:36:13,340
( laughs )
How would you?

974
00:36:13,830 --> 00:36:14,830
Gene! Gene!

975
00:36:14,840 --> 00:36:16,840
He just
doesn't care.

976
00:36:16,840 --> 00:36:21,340
Yeah, you'd have to breathe
them dry to find out.

977
00:36:21,340 --> 00:36:22,850
No one's
ever done that.

978
00:36:22,840 --> 00:36:26,340
Well, I got the feeling
we just may be the first.

979
00:36:26,350 --> 00:36:28,350
Oh, look, they're doing
the Hindu rope trick.

980
00:36:28,350 --> 00:36:29,850
Whoo.

981
00:36:29,850 --> 00:36:32,860
( sirens )

982
00:36:32,850 --> 00:36:35,850
From "Marooned"
to Gator McKlusky.

983
00:36:35,860 --> 00:36:37,360
( laughs )

984
00:36:37,360 --> 00:36:39,860
We now return
to "Cannonball Run."

985
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:43,370
( all humming )

986
00:36:44,870 --> 00:36:46,870
So I guess
the point here is,

987
00:36:46,870 --> 00:36:48,870
that these people
are driving somewhere.

988
00:36:48,870 --> 00:36:50,370
Mm-hmm.

989
00:36:51,870 --> 00:36:54,380
Capra, Jr. said
they bought the crane,

990
00:36:54,370 --> 00:36:56,370
so they're gonna use it,
and here they go.

991
00:36:56,380 --> 00:36:58,380
Now, everybody remember,

992
00:36:58,380 --> 00:37:00,880
we're parking in the
turtle lot, the turtle lot.

993
00:37:02,380 --> 00:37:04,380
"Hell Fighters" with
John Wayne will return.

994
00:37:04,890 --> 00:37:06,390
( all humming )

995
00:37:06,390 --> 00:37:08,390
It's George Bush.
Check it out.

996
00:37:10,890 --> 00:37:13,900
Hey, look, they set up
a couple of wet bars.

997
00:37:17,900 --> 00:37:20,900
It's Bolt versus Blaze
in an all-out battle,

998
00:37:20,900 --> 00:37:23,910
tonight on
"American Gladiators!"

999
00:37:23,900 --> 00:37:26,900
- Jump! Jump! Jump!
- Jump! Jump! Jump!

1000
00:37:26,910 --> 00:37:28,910
Jump!

1001
00:37:28,910 --> 00:37:31,410
This is Ground Control
to Major Tom.

1002
00:37:31,410 --> 00:37:32,920
You've really made the grade

1003
00:37:32,910 --> 00:37:33,910
and the papers want to know

1004
00:37:33,910 --> 00:37:36,410
whose shirts you wear.
Over.

1005
00:37:36,420 --> 00:37:37,920
( laughs )

1006
00:37:37,920 --> 00:37:40,920
Pull the GNN
circuit breakers and
the IMU heater breakers.

1007
00:37:40,920 --> 00:37:43,430
You do it.
I'm bitter.

1008
00:37:43,420 --> 00:37:45,420
Shut down the attitude
control system.

1009
00:37:45,430 --> 00:37:46,930
Oh, the attitude
control.

1010
00:37:46,930 --> 00:37:47,930
SCS secured.

1011
00:37:47,930 --> 00:37:50,430
Shut down number two
and three fuel cells.

1012
00:37:50,430 --> 00:37:51,930
We'll leave number one
on the line

1013
00:37:51,930 --> 00:37:53,440
in case we have to
charge the batteries.

1014
00:37:53,430 --> 00:37:54,430
Check and see
if I unplugged

1015
00:37:54,430 --> 00:37:55,930
the curling iron,
too, would you?

1016
00:37:55,940 --> 00:37:56,940
- What about the comm?
- No, we'll leave it on.

1017
00:37:56,940 --> 00:37:57,940
Flood lights off.

1018
00:37:57,940 --> 00:37:59,440
You guys
are great to be with.

1019
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:00,940
Can we stay up
and talk a while?

1020
00:38:00,940 --> 00:38:04,950
- EL off.
- Yel-low.

1021
00:38:04,950 --> 00:38:06,950
Buzz, the lower
equipment bays dark?

1022
00:38:06,950 --> 00:38:08,450
Well, the B lights
are off.

1023
00:38:08,450 --> 00:38:11,450
Good night, Mrs. Calabash,
wherever you are.

1024
00:38:11,450 --> 00:38:12,960
And why isn't that off?

1025
00:38:12,950 --> 00:38:14,450
It's my nightlight.

1026
00:38:14,450 --> 00:38:15,950
I don't know why.

1027
00:38:15,960 --> 00:38:17,960
Gene, can I have
a drink of water?

1028
00:38:19,960 --> 00:38:22,970
Houston, Ironman,
we're cranked down.

1029
00:38:22,960 --> 00:38:25,460
Copy, Jim.

1030
00:38:25,470 --> 00:38:28,470
We'll check in
when you come over
the hill at Guaymas.

1031
00:38:28,470 --> 00:38:30,470
We're gonna keep you
informed every step
of the way.

1032
00:38:30,470 --> 00:38:31,970
Every movie you make,
every step you take,

1033
00:38:31,970 --> 00:38:33,480
we'll be watching you.

1034
00:38:33,470 --> 00:38:35,470
- In a while.
- Jim, this is Courtney.

1035
00:38:35,480 --> 00:38:37,480
Now, I want you to take
one tablet of Stryzene
after every meal.

1036
00:38:37,480 --> 00:38:39,480
- Who wants gum?
- I do! I do!

1037
00:38:39,480 --> 00:38:40,480
Complete the power down.

1038
00:38:40,980 --> 00:38:41,980
You can drink as much
water as you want.

1039
00:38:42,480 --> 00:38:45,480
Now, every word, every
emotion costs oxygen,

1040
00:38:45,490 --> 00:38:47,490
so the more relaxed
you are, the better.

1041
00:38:47,490 --> 00:38:51,490
All right, you bums, take
your pills and go to sleep.

1042
00:38:51,490 --> 00:38:52,500
Oh, jeez, he's drunk again.

1043
00:38:52,490 --> 00:38:53,990
Hey! Hello,
big brother.

1044
00:38:53,990 --> 00:38:55,990
- Hiya, Ted.
- Hiya, Stoney.

1045
00:38:56,000 --> 00:38:59,000
- Hey, where are you?
- I'm at Lucy's house.

1046
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:02,000
Well, I'm in Huntsville,
kludging up a simulator
to the XRV.

1047
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:04,010
I'm at Space Camp
with Kate Capshaw.

1048
00:39:04,000 --> 00:39:05,500
A simulator to the XRV.

1049
00:39:05,510 --> 00:39:06,510
I'm gonna do a little
brush up here

1050
00:39:06,510 --> 00:39:08,510
before I take it
to the Cape.

1051
00:39:08,510 --> 00:39:10,010
Well, this is a great time

1052
00:39:10,010 --> 00:39:11,010
to start learning
how to fly it.

1053
00:39:11,010 --> 00:39:12,520
Listen, all you guys
gotta worry about

1054
00:39:12,510 --> 00:39:14,510
is where you want me
to set you down

1055
00:39:14,510 --> 00:39:16,510
after I make
the transfer.

1056
00:39:16,520 --> 00:39:18,520
Go on, pick your spot.
Edwards? Vegas?

1057
00:39:18,520 --> 00:39:20,020
How about
the beach in Malibu?

1058
00:39:20,020 --> 00:39:21,520
Why not?

1059
00:39:21,520 --> 00:39:23,530
All right, take your
pills, go to sleep,

1060
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:25,020
and leave
the driving to us.

1061
00:39:25,030 --> 00:39:26,530
Good night.
I'm out of here!

1062
00:39:26,530 --> 00:39:29,530
I'll see you in 39 hours
and 16 minutes.

1063
00:39:29,530 --> 00:39:31,530
Not if we see you first.
( laughs )

1064
00:39:31,530 --> 00:39:33,040
I love that one.
That's really good.

1065
00:39:35,040 --> 00:39:38,040
Tastes like fresh cherries.

1066
00:39:38,040 --> 00:39:40,540
I wonder what mine's
gonna taste like?

1067
00:39:40,540 --> 00:39:43,550
Mmm. ( gulps )

1068
00:39:48,050 --> 00:39:50,550
Gene, stop looking at it
and just eat it!

1069
00:39:50,550 --> 00:39:52,560
What's he doing?

1070
00:39:52,550 --> 00:39:55,550
He's crushing it into
his cleft on his chin.

1071
00:39:55,560 --> 00:39:58,060
A mortar and pestle.

1072
00:39:58,060 --> 00:40:01,060
Time to go.
Shoes for industry.

1073
00:40:01,060 --> 00:40:03,070
Shoes for the dead.
Shoes for industry.

1074
00:40:03,060 --> 00:40:05,060
Hi, there. My name's
Joe Beats.

1075
00:40:05,070 --> 00:40:06,570
Say, what chance
does that--

1076
00:40:16,380 --> 00:40:18,880
Ironman One, Ironman One.
This is Houston.

1077
00:40:18,880 --> 00:40:19,880
Do you read me?

1078
00:40:19,880 --> 00:40:20,880
Roger that, Houston.

1079
00:40:21,380 --> 00:40:22,380
Coming in
on the wall-to-wall.

1080
00:40:22,380 --> 00:40:23,890
Roger that, Ironman.

1081
00:40:23,880 --> 00:40:25,380
Advise you adjust
your attitude

1082
00:40:25,390 --> 00:40:27,890
using happy hour
protocol 3-niner.

1083
00:40:27,890 --> 00:40:29,390
Roger that, 3-niner.

1084
00:40:29,390 --> 00:40:31,390
Houston, we're gonna
highball the Delta V
to modify attitude.

1085
00:40:31,390 --> 00:40:33,400
Roger, we have
cranked down on the IMU,

1086
00:40:33,390 --> 00:40:34,890
you-are-me, tuck
and roll formation.

1087
00:40:34,890 --> 00:40:38,390
Roger, Corman,
advise you pull all
GNN circuit breakers,

1088
00:40:38,400 --> 00:40:40,400
modify your ILM Skywalkers,

1089
00:40:40,400 --> 00:40:43,910
and full retraction
of your Robin Lame
Chartbusters

1090
00:40:43,900 --> 00:40:46,400
and hold on your power
and glory switches.

1091
00:40:46,410 --> 00:40:47,910
Roger on that
glory hold, sir.

1092
00:40:47,910 --> 00:40:48,910
Hold, I read Gypsy, doctor.

1093
00:40:48,910 --> 00:40:50,910
Serious start up leak
in apathy thrusters.

1094
00:40:50,910 --> 00:40:51,910
Serious yaw,
pitch and kick

1095
00:40:51,910 --> 00:40:53,420
with apathy flux and rate.

1096
00:40:53,410 --> 00:40:54,910
Roger. Stay calm, boys.

1097
00:40:54,910 --> 00:40:57,910
Shut down all SVS dissident
controls but number one.

1098
00:40:57,920 --> 00:40:59,420
Speaking of number one,
Stoney,

1099
00:40:59,420 --> 00:41:01,420
I got too much Tang in me.
I got renal overflow.

1100
00:41:01,920 --> 00:41:03,430
Better send up that
john, Houston.

1101
00:41:03,920 --> 00:41:05,420
Looks like Hackman here
has screwed the pooch.
Over.

1102
00:41:05,430 --> 00:41:06,930
Roger that, pooch.

1103
00:41:06,930 --> 00:41:08,930
We're cranked down
till 0-niner-fiver.

1104
00:41:08,930 --> 00:41:10,930
Check in when you come over
the hill at Cannery.

1105
00:41:10,930 --> 00:41:12,430
That's Canary, Crow.

1106
00:41:12,430 --> 00:41:14,440
Right, boys,
so just take your pills

1107
00:41:14,430 --> 00:41:15,930
and kiss
your apogee good-bye.

1108
00:41:15,940 --> 00:41:16,940
- What?
- What? What are you--

1109
00:41:17,440 --> 00:41:18,940
That's right,
this is Fugitive One.

1110
00:41:18,940 --> 00:41:20,940
When you want
headache relief fast,

1111
00:41:20,940 --> 00:41:21,940
two Bufferin
and a glass of scotch.

1112
00:41:21,940 --> 00:41:23,450
No, no! Joel!
He's wrecking everything.

1113
00:41:23,440 --> 00:41:24,440
- Crow, what are you doing?
- What are you doing there?

1114
00:41:24,440 --> 00:41:27,440
I'm just trying
to spice it up.

1115
00:41:27,450 --> 00:41:28,450
This here is The Fugitive

1116
00:41:28,450 --> 00:41:29,950
looking for
the riverboat queen,

1117
00:41:29,950 --> 00:41:32,450
hauling a load
of sailboat fuel
northbound on the nickel.

1118
00:41:32,450 --> 00:41:33,460
You got a bear
in your ear and a--

1119
00:41:33,450 --> 00:41:35,950
Crow! Crow, enough
with the CB lingo.

1120
00:41:35,960 --> 00:41:38,960
The only reason we did
this is because you
do such a killer Peck.

1121
00:41:38,960 --> 00:41:40,460
10-4, killer Peck.

1122
00:41:40,460 --> 00:41:42,460
You got some
rusty load running east
out of Needletown.

1123
00:41:42,960 --> 00:41:44,460
Looking at
the flabby gas passer,

1124
00:41:44,960 --> 00:41:46,960
I'll be 10-10
on the side of fries--

1125
00:41:46,970 --> 00:41:48,470
Joel: Come on.
You're coming with me

1126
00:41:48,470 --> 00:41:50,970
Crow: ♪ Riverboat queen,
coming in to Los Angeles ♪

1127
00:41:51,470 --> 00:41:52,470
♪ Bringing in
a couple of keys ♪

1128
00:41:52,970 --> 00:41:54,970
Open the pod bay door, Hal.
Whoa, movie sign!

1129
00:41:54,970 --> 00:41:56,970
- ( alarm )
- Help me. I'm stuck. Oh!

1130
00:42:07,490 --> 00:42:08,490
♪ Don't touch my bag
if you please ♪

1131
00:42:08,490 --> 00:42:09,990
Crow ruined everything.

1132
00:42:09,990 --> 00:42:11,990
I hate it when
you leave me hanging

1133
00:42:11,990 --> 00:42:13,500
in the middle
of skits like that.

1134
00:42:13,490 --> 00:42:15,990
I should caution you.

1135
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:18,000
It's the beautiful
Breck girls.

1136
00:42:18,000 --> 00:42:20,000
You're gonna notice
some degeneration.

1137
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:21,000
Is that good?

1138
00:42:21,000 --> 00:42:23,010
But it's important to show
them how confident you are.

1139
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:24,000
We understand, Dick.

1140
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:25,000
We won't let you down.

1141
00:42:25,000 --> 00:42:26,500
You want us
to be confident?

1142
00:42:26,510 --> 00:42:30,010
The more relaxed they are,
the less oxygen they use.

1143
00:42:30,010 --> 00:42:32,510
- I know why we're here.
- You do?

1144
00:42:32,510 --> 00:42:34,020
You're letting us
say good-bye.

1145
00:42:34,010 --> 00:42:35,510
Is that Lou Grant?

1146
00:42:35,520 --> 00:42:37,520
No, no, no. Lee Grant.

1147
00:42:37,520 --> 00:42:38,520
Lee Press-On Grant.

1148
00:42:38,520 --> 00:42:40,020
Celia?

1149
00:42:41,020 --> 00:42:44,030
Lee, honey, that's a Fall
if ever I saw one.

1150
00:42:46,030 --> 00:42:50,030
Jiggle the antenna.
Put it on channel 3.

1151
00:42:50,030 --> 00:42:52,030
Put some tin--
There you go.

1152
00:42:52,030 --> 00:42:54,040
- Hi, Celia.
- Hi, Frank.

1153
00:42:54,030 --> 00:42:56,030
Celia?

1154
00:42:57,040 --> 00:42:58,540
Oh! Nothing, nothing!

1155
00:42:58,540 --> 00:43:01,040
Wasn't touching.
It's okay.

1156
00:43:01,040 --> 00:43:03,550
Celia?

1157
00:43:05,550 --> 00:43:07,050
- Celia?
- You're breaking my heart.

1158
00:43:07,050 --> 00:43:08,550
- Celia?
- You're breaking my heart.

1159
00:43:08,550 --> 00:43:10,050
Celia, we're gonna talk to him
on the monitor up here.

1160
00:43:10,050 --> 00:43:11,050
Okay.

1161
00:43:15,050 --> 00:43:16,550
What are you
laughing at?

1162
00:43:16,560 --> 00:43:20,060
You know, in those days,
you were required to smoke.

1163
00:43:24,560 --> 00:43:30,560
Honey, guess where
I'm calling from?

1164
00:43:30,570 --> 00:43:32,570
Celia.

1165
00:43:32,570 --> 00:43:34,580
Well, here I am again.

1166
00:43:34,570 --> 00:43:38,070
Signed, sealed, delivered,
I'm yours.

1167
00:43:38,080 --> 00:43:39,580
What are you wearing?

1168
00:43:39,580 --> 00:43:41,580
Everybody's so nice.

1169
00:43:41,580 --> 00:43:43,090
Sometime.

1170
00:43:43,080 --> 00:43:46,080
I spoke
to your father today.

1171
00:43:46,590 --> 00:43:48,090
- Uh-oh.
- And he says to say hello.

1172
00:43:48,590 --> 00:43:50,590
And he says he's sorry
you're going to die--

1173
00:43:50,590 --> 00:43:52,090
- I mean--
- Shh.

1174
00:43:52,090 --> 00:43:55,100
I bought some new shoes.

1175
00:43:55,090 --> 00:43:57,590
- Shoes. Huh.
- They hurt.

1176
00:43:57,600 --> 00:44:00,100
That's great.
What the heck does that
have to do with anything?

1177
00:44:00,100 --> 00:44:01,100
I'm dying up here!

1178
00:44:01,100 --> 00:44:03,610
- Kids?
- Fine, fine.

1179
00:44:03,600 --> 00:44:07,600
I lost the bill
to the insurance.

1180
00:44:07,610 --> 00:44:08,610
I thought I had it paid,

1181
00:44:08,610 --> 00:44:10,610
and then just this
morning, I got a letter.

1182
00:44:10,610 --> 00:44:12,110
Huh, I'd find that
and pay it
if I were you.

1183
00:44:12,610 --> 00:44:16,620
Listen, babe, I just want
to tell you that I love you.

1184
00:44:16,620 --> 00:44:18,620
What?

1185
00:44:20,620 --> 00:44:23,130
I said I love you.

1186
00:44:23,120 --> 00:44:24,120
In front of the guys?

1187
00:44:24,120 --> 00:44:27,120
I just wanted you
to know.

1188
00:44:27,630 --> 00:44:30,130
Mm-hmm, neat. Now, so
the house needs painting

1189
00:44:30,130 --> 00:44:32,630
and I was wondering if
you'd like a nice eggshell
or maybe an enamel?

1190
00:44:32,630 --> 00:44:34,140
I just wanted
to make sure.

1191
00:44:34,130 --> 00:44:37,630
Stoner, come on,
you're hurting my legs.
Scooch over.

1192
00:44:37,640 --> 00:44:39,640
Is it very lonely
where you are?

1193
00:44:40,140 --> 00:44:41,140
Nah, not with these guys.

1194
00:44:41,140 --> 00:44:43,650
Stoney's rigging
a beer bong.

1195
00:44:43,640 --> 00:44:48,140
No, I'm not.
Not for long.

1196
00:44:48,150 --> 00:44:50,150
We all have
great confidence.

1197
00:44:50,650 --> 00:44:54,160
That your death will be
quick and painless. Oops.

1198
00:44:54,150 --> 00:44:58,150
Celia, if you have,
you know,

1199
00:44:58,160 --> 00:44:59,660
any more problems
with the insurance

1200
00:44:59,660 --> 00:45:01,660
or the house
or anything like that,

1201
00:45:01,660 --> 00:45:04,170
you can talk to Dougherty.

1202
00:45:04,160 --> 00:45:05,660
- He's a good guy.
- And he's alive.

1203
00:45:05,670 --> 00:45:07,170
You can depend on him.

1204
00:45:07,170 --> 00:45:10,170
I gave him a kiss
to give you
when he sees you.

1205
00:45:10,670 --> 00:45:12,170
Just how many kisses
did you give him?

1206
00:45:12,170 --> 00:45:13,680
Just so when I see him,
I'll know.

1207
00:45:13,670 --> 00:45:15,670
Yeah, just what I need.

1208
00:45:15,680 --> 00:45:17,680
( laughs )
You never can tell.

1209
00:45:17,680 --> 00:45:20,180
( laughs )
A little tuned up, honey.

1210
00:45:22,180 --> 00:45:23,690
Uh-huh.

1211
00:45:23,680 --> 00:45:26,180
So I like pudding.

1212
00:45:28,190 --> 00:45:30,190
Well, I gotta go now.

1213
00:45:30,190 --> 00:45:32,190
- No, not yet.
- Where?

1214
00:45:34,190 --> 00:45:38,690
No, I mean, I really
have to go now.

1215
00:45:45,710 --> 00:45:48,210
Celia, I, uh...

1216
00:45:48,210 --> 00:45:51,210
I had a real good day today.

1217
00:45:51,210 --> 00:45:53,220
I made an ashtray.

1218
00:45:53,210 --> 00:45:59,210
And, you know, when you
think about this, uh...

1219
00:45:59,220 --> 00:46:01,720
And you will think about
this, please be kind.

1220
00:46:01,720 --> 00:46:04,730
I want you to remember
I had a real good day.

1221
00:46:04,720 --> 00:46:08,220
You're not giving me much
to work with, honey.

1222
00:46:08,230 --> 00:46:09,230
Jim...

1223
00:46:09,230 --> 00:46:12,230
( gulping )

1224
00:46:12,230 --> 00:46:15,740
We better cut it off now.

1225
00:46:15,740 --> 00:46:17,740
Will I see you in September

1226
00:46:17,740 --> 00:46:19,240
or lose you
to a summer love?

1227
00:46:19,240 --> 00:46:20,740
Oh, there's
one more thing.

1228
00:46:20,740 --> 00:46:22,740
I'm buying a--
well, I guess it can
wait until your dead--

1229
00:46:22,740 --> 00:46:24,750
I mean down. Sorry.

1230
00:46:24,740 --> 00:46:27,240
I lost five pounds
last week.

1231
00:46:27,250 --> 00:46:29,750
I can't wait
till you see me.

1232
00:46:29,750 --> 00:46:31,250
So? Big deal.
I'm weightless.

1233
00:46:35,250 --> 00:46:38,750
That tickles, honey.
Don't do that.

1234
00:46:38,760 --> 00:46:40,260
I'm too good,
in fact.

1235
00:46:40,260 --> 00:46:42,260
You take care of yourself.

1236
00:46:42,260 --> 00:46:44,770
Take care.

1237
00:46:46,770 --> 00:46:48,770
( sobbing )

1238
00:46:50,270 --> 00:46:51,770
And you're healed,
honey.

1239
00:46:51,770 --> 00:46:55,280
Note to myself,
remember the monitor thing
when he comes home.

1240
00:46:57,280 --> 00:46:59,280
I'm gonna be doing
TV movies

1241
00:46:59,280 --> 00:47:02,780
for the rest of my career.
( sobbing )

1242
00:47:02,780 --> 00:47:05,290
Celia, how'd it go
with Dick?

1243
00:47:05,280 --> 00:47:07,780
Does he know about us?

1244
00:47:08,290 --> 00:47:11,290
Could you touch
my screen, too?
Come on, Celia.

1245
00:47:11,290 --> 00:47:13,300
Hiya, sport.

1246
00:47:13,290 --> 00:47:14,290
Hiya, kid.

1247
00:47:14,290 --> 00:47:16,290
How are the mice?

1248
00:47:16,800 --> 00:47:18,300
Oh, they're dead.
How's the rabbit?

1249
00:47:18,300 --> 00:47:20,800
We're learning
something every day.

1250
00:47:20,800 --> 00:47:23,310
You writing a big
dull book up there?

1251
00:47:23,300 --> 00:47:25,300
No. I leave that
to James Michener.

1252
00:47:25,810 --> 00:47:28,810
Just a small, dull book.
How are you?

1253
00:47:28,810 --> 00:47:30,810
Gorgeous. And you?

1254
00:47:30,810 --> 00:47:33,820
I'm working on
a little project.

1255
00:47:33,810 --> 00:47:38,810
I've developed an
experimental hypothesis
for you to proof out.

1256
00:47:38,820 --> 00:47:42,320
Have you?
Well, what is it?

1257
00:47:42,320 --> 00:47:43,830
How long can you
hold your breath?

1258
00:47:43,820 --> 00:47:45,320
( whispering )
I miss you like mad.

1259
00:47:47,330 --> 00:47:49,830
Grrr. Yum, yum,
yum, yum.

1260
00:47:49,830 --> 00:47:51,830
Yeah, I love you,
too, honey.

1261
00:47:51,830 --> 00:47:55,340
Your paper
came out last week.

1262
00:47:55,330 --> 00:47:56,830
Oh, you got a B-.

1263
00:47:56,840 --> 00:47:59,840
Professor Stavel said it's
a brilliant presentation.

1264
00:47:59,840 --> 00:48:01,840
It was the best
he's ever read.

1265
00:48:01,840 --> 00:48:03,850
We're gonna make it,
Teresa.

1266
00:48:03,840 --> 00:48:05,340
- What?
- Huh?

1267
00:48:05,340 --> 00:48:06,340
We're gonna make it back.

1268
00:48:06,350 --> 00:48:07,850
- What?
- Huh?

1269
00:48:07,850 --> 00:48:09,350
We're going
to make it back.

1270
00:48:09,350 --> 00:48:10,850
Oh, that's great.
Now about the insurance.

1271
00:48:10,850 --> 00:48:12,350
I was wondering
if I have to--

1272
00:48:12,350 --> 00:48:14,360
- Believe me.
- Yes.

1273
00:48:14,350 --> 00:48:16,850
I'm mean it.
We're all coming back.

1274
00:48:16,860 --> 00:48:19,860
Back? Well...well...
could...when?

1275
00:48:19,860 --> 00:48:21,860
Could you call
first?

1276
00:48:23,860 --> 00:48:25,860
Listen, Teresa, I--

1277
00:48:25,870 --> 00:48:26,870
It's Teresa.

1278
00:48:26,870 --> 00:48:29,870
I want you
and the kids to--

1279
00:48:29,870 --> 00:48:32,370
Oh, wait, I've got
another call coming in.

1280
00:48:32,370 --> 00:48:34,880
Could you hold a minute? Uh.

1281
00:48:34,870 --> 00:48:37,870
Look, I have to go now.

1282
00:48:37,880 --> 00:48:39,880
I'll be seeing you.

1283
00:48:39,880 --> 00:48:40,880
Stoney.

1284
00:48:40,880 --> 00:48:41,880
I'll be seeing you.

1285
00:48:41,880 --> 00:48:43,890
- No, you hang up first.
- No, you hang up.

1286
00:48:43,880 --> 00:48:45,880
- No, you hang up.
- No, you.

1287
00:48:45,890 --> 00:48:48,890
I love you.
Remember that.

1288
00:48:48,890 --> 00:48:50,390
What was
the middle part?

1289
00:48:50,390 --> 00:48:52,390
Remember that.

1290
00:48:53,390 --> 00:48:56,390
We'll always have
Encino. Whatever.

1291
00:48:56,400 --> 00:48:59,900
Oh, fudge,
he was my connection.

1292
00:48:59,900 --> 00:49:02,900
( blubbering )

1293
00:49:02,900 --> 00:49:05,910
This makes for great TV.

1294
00:49:05,910 --> 00:49:07,910
Buzz?

1295
00:49:07,910 --> 00:49:11,410
Ah, what? Nothing.
I wasn't doing...just...

1296
00:49:11,410 --> 00:49:14,420
General LeClair
called all the way
from Clark's Field.

1297
00:49:14,410 --> 00:49:15,910
He asked me to dinner.

1298
00:49:15,920 --> 00:49:17,420
I thought
that was so nice.

1299
00:49:17,420 --> 00:49:18,920
Oh, and Jim Garner
called, too.

1300
00:49:18,920 --> 00:49:21,420
They want to
give you something.

1301
00:49:21,420 --> 00:49:22,920
A headstone.

1302
00:49:22,920 --> 00:49:26,430
Look, hon, you don't
seem to understand.

1303
00:49:26,430 --> 00:49:29,430
It's all falling apart
up here. You understand?

1304
00:49:29,430 --> 00:49:30,930
Well, that's nice, honey.

1305
00:49:30,930 --> 00:49:32,430
I understand, Buzz.

1306
00:49:32,430 --> 00:49:35,940
You see, I broke
the washing machine,

1307
00:49:35,940 --> 00:49:37,940
and now I can't fix it.

1308
00:49:37,940 --> 00:49:39,940
What is he, the Maytag
repairman all of a sudden?

1309
00:49:40,440 --> 00:49:41,940
I understand
how you feel.

1310
00:49:41,940 --> 00:49:44,450
I could fix it if they'd
give me the tools,

1311
00:49:44,440 --> 00:49:46,440
but they won't.

1312
00:49:46,450 --> 00:49:49,450
Listen, honey,
I wrote something.

1313
00:49:49,450 --> 00:49:52,450
It's called
"The Reluctant Astronaut
Bites the Big One."

1314
00:49:52,450 --> 00:49:53,960
- Do you want to hear it?
- What?

1315
00:49:53,950 --> 00:49:57,950
The President,
the White House,
Washington D.C.

1316
00:49:57,960 --> 00:49:59,960
Dear Mr. President,

1317
00:49:59,960 --> 00:50:00,960
We just wanted
to let you know...

1318
00:50:00,960 --> 00:50:02,460
She's making this up.

1319
00:50:02,460 --> 00:50:05,470
How much we appreciate
the heroic actions
to save our husbands.

1320
00:50:05,460 --> 00:50:08,960
We are grateful to you
and to everyone
in the nation.

1321
00:50:08,970 --> 00:50:11,470
The team effort
of the past two days

1322
00:50:11,470 --> 00:50:13,480
will be an inspiration
to us, our children...

1323
00:50:13,470 --> 00:50:14,970
God, this is so
poorly written. Jeez.

1324
00:50:14,970 --> 00:50:16,470
Don't you understand?

1325
00:50:16,480 --> 00:50:17,980
It's all falling apart
and they're blaming me

1326
00:50:18,480 --> 00:50:22,480
and I don't feel very good.

1327
00:50:22,480 --> 00:50:25,990
Can I fix you a nice 7-Up
and soda crackers, dear?

1328
00:50:25,990 --> 00:50:29,990
Buzz...

1329
00:50:29,990 --> 00:50:30,990
I have to go now.

1330
00:50:30,990 --> 00:50:32,490
Bye.

1331
00:50:32,490 --> 00:50:36,500
I love you.
I'll see you later.

1332
00:50:36,500 --> 00:50:39,500
Bye, honey. Guy's
a five-pound fruitcake.

1333
00:50:39,500 --> 00:50:41,000
I gotta get out of here.

1334
00:50:41,000 --> 00:50:42,000
The other husbands
were so strong

1335
00:50:42,000 --> 00:50:43,000
and mine's
a tower of Jell-O,

1336
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:45,010
but he's good, though.
Hmm.

1337
00:50:47,010 --> 00:50:50,010
Sorry, everyone, sorry.
Oh, turn him off.

1338
00:50:50,010 --> 00:50:51,010
Buzz, can you give me
a reading

1339
00:50:51,510 --> 00:50:53,520
on your partial pressure
of carbon dioxide?

1340
00:50:53,510 --> 00:50:55,510
- ( inhales ) Here.
- Thank you very much.

1341
00:50:55,510 --> 00:50:57,010
I just gave you that!

1342
00:50:57,020 --> 00:50:58,520
Turn it off!

1343
00:50:58,520 --> 00:51:00,020
- Negative, Buzz.
- Turn it off!

1344
00:51:00,020 --> 00:51:02,020
Negative.
You gave me a PS--

1345
00:51:02,020 --> 00:51:03,530
This is good TV.

1346
00:51:03,520 --> 00:51:06,020
What's going on
down there?

1347
00:51:06,030 --> 00:51:08,030
Why don't you get us
out of here?

1348
00:51:08,030 --> 00:51:09,030
Get a hold of him, Jim.

1349
00:51:09,030 --> 00:51:11,030
Get him away
from the controls!

1350
00:51:11,030 --> 00:51:12,030
Why don't you come up here
and get us out of here?

1351
00:51:12,030 --> 00:51:13,540
Sedate him.

1352
00:51:13,530 --> 00:51:15,530
Put on some Dan Fogelberg.

1353
00:51:15,530 --> 00:51:18,030
Buzz? Come on.

1354
00:51:18,040 --> 00:51:19,540
- Buzz.
- Oh, no.

1355
00:51:19,540 --> 00:51:21,540
He thinks he's Shatner,
for crying out loud.

1356
00:51:21,540 --> 00:51:25,050
Now just get us out of here,
that's all! Come on!

1357
00:51:25,040 --> 00:51:27,040
- Buzz off.
- I'll say.

1358
00:51:27,050 --> 00:51:29,550
That's my man
up there.

1359
00:51:29,550 --> 00:51:33,560
You better have a woman's
lounge around here.

1360
00:51:41,290 --> 00:51:43,790
I've got points
on this game.

1361
00:51:43,800 --> 00:51:45,800
I can't believe
you bet on the Bears.

1362
00:51:47,300 --> 00:51:49,800
It is cold out there.

1363
00:51:49,800 --> 00:51:53,810
T-minus 60 seconds
and counting.

1364
00:51:53,810 --> 00:51:55,810
- Hold!
- Hold!

1365
00:51:57,310 --> 00:51:58,810
I'm gonna do
what Greg does.

1366
00:51:58,810 --> 00:52:00,320
Shut her down.

1367
00:52:00,310 --> 00:52:01,810
Damn.

1368
00:52:01,810 --> 00:52:04,310
Tower crew to launch pad.
Move in, Tower.

1369
00:52:06,320 --> 00:52:09,320
But for Vince Lombardi
and the Green Bay Packers,

1370
00:52:09,320 --> 00:52:11,330
there would come
another day.

1371
00:52:13,330 --> 00:52:16,330
Gene? Gene, buddy,
chin up.

1372
00:52:16,330 --> 00:52:18,330
Hey.

1373
00:52:18,330 --> 00:52:19,330
Oh, man.

1374
00:52:19,330 --> 00:52:21,340
Houston, Ironman,
do you read?

1375
00:52:23,840 --> 00:52:26,340
I can't read.
That's my secret shame.

1376
00:52:26,340 --> 00:52:27,840
Goldstone, this is Ironman.
Do you read?

1377
00:52:27,840 --> 00:52:30,850
Goldstone,
this is Kidney Stone,
gallstone niner.

1378
00:52:32,340 --> 00:52:34,340
I know you're there.
Stop screening your calls.

1379
00:52:34,350 --> 00:52:36,350
Guaymas, Ironman.

1380
00:52:36,350 --> 00:52:38,350
Rubber ply,
this is Iron John.

1381
00:52:38,350 --> 00:52:42,360
Gene, what does your suit
smell like, buddy? ( laughs )

1382
00:52:42,350 --> 00:52:44,350
Cape, this is Ironman.

1383
00:52:44,360 --> 00:52:45,360
Pruett?

1384
00:52:45,360 --> 00:52:46,860
I'm sorry, I thought Jim
was gonna get it

1385
00:52:46,860 --> 00:52:48,360
and he thought
I was gonna get it.

1386
00:52:48,360 --> 00:52:51,370
I'm sorry. We had
to scrub the launch.

1387
00:52:51,360 --> 00:52:52,860
Hey, hey, hey,
there's ladies present.

1388
00:52:54,870 --> 00:52:57,870
These guys better
scrub the inside
of their suits.

1389
00:52:59,370 --> 00:53:02,380
Yeah.

1390
00:53:02,370 --> 00:53:03,870
Yeah, I figured
it might be like that.

1391
00:53:03,880 --> 00:53:06,880
We've been
watching the storm.

1392
00:53:06,880 --> 00:53:10,890
We suggest you start
breathing in shifts.

1393
00:53:10,880 --> 00:53:13,380
T-minus 60 seconds.

1394
00:53:13,390 --> 00:53:15,390
Now, listen, Jim,
we're still working
on that engine.

1395
00:53:15,390 --> 00:53:16,890
Oh, you got the engine
down there?

1396
00:53:16,890 --> 00:53:19,390
No wonder it doesn't work.

1397
00:53:19,390 --> 00:53:23,900
Oh, great. Now
there's a bank robbery.

1398
00:53:28,400 --> 00:53:30,410
Oh, this bites,
Houston. Over.

1399
00:53:30,400 --> 00:53:35,900
Boss, I just...

1400
00:53:36,410 --> 00:53:37,910
I wanted to tell you

1401
00:53:38,410 --> 00:53:39,910
that you've done
a great job.

1402
00:53:40,410 --> 00:53:41,910
Well, up till now
at any rate.

1403
00:53:41,910 --> 00:53:44,410
And, uh...

1404
00:53:44,420 --> 00:53:45,920
Well, no hard feelings.

1405
00:53:45,920 --> 00:53:47,420
The place looks great
and you look great

1406
00:53:47,420 --> 00:53:49,420
and, well, you know.

1407
00:53:49,420 --> 00:53:50,930
I just want to say...

1408
00:53:50,920 --> 00:53:54,920
♪ I'm so glad we had
this time together ♪

1409
00:53:55,430 --> 00:53:57,930
Now you've done
a great job, really.

1410
00:53:58,430 --> 00:54:00,440
I'd like to be fed
to my dog.

1411
00:54:00,430 --> 00:54:02,430
It's nobody's fault.

1412
00:54:02,430 --> 00:54:03,430
Good night now.

1413
00:54:03,440 --> 00:54:06,940
GBI reports loss of signal.

1414
00:54:07,440 --> 00:54:08,940
Ascension will acquire
the spacecraft.

1415
00:54:09,440 --> 00:54:10,450
Shall we remote
you through?

1416
00:54:12,440 --> 00:54:13,940
No.

1417
00:54:13,950 --> 00:54:15,450
Move in, white room.
Get the pilot out of there.

1418
00:54:15,450 --> 00:54:16,950
It's Storming Norman.
Check it out.

1419
00:54:16,950 --> 00:54:20,460
Now get me Keith.
I want to speak to Keith.

1420
00:54:20,450 --> 00:54:21,450
Talk to me,
sweetcakes.

1421
00:54:21,950 --> 00:54:24,450
We've done everything
we possibly could.

1422
00:54:24,460 --> 00:54:26,460
We cannot launch.

1423
00:54:26,460 --> 00:54:28,460
But I'm telling you
to launch!

1424
00:54:28,460 --> 00:54:29,960
You get this hay wagon
off the ground!

1425
00:54:29,960 --> 00:54:31,470
No, no,
I can't hear you.

1426
00:54:31,460 --> 00:54:32,460
I'm can't--
I'm leaving.

1427
00:54:32,460 --> 00:54:34,460
La-la-la-la.
I do not hear you.

1428
00:54:34,470 --> 00:54:36,970
La-la-la-la.
I do not hear you.

1429
00:54:36,970 --> 00:54:38,970
Okay. Stay
with the group.

1430
00:54:38,970 --> 00:54:41,980
We promise you'll see
some stars this tour.

1431
00:54:41,970 --> 00:54:43,970
In one of these buildings,
there may be a hurricane.

1432
00:54:44,480 --> 00:54:45,980
Look, there's
Gregory Peck.

1433
00:54:45,980 --> 00:54:46,980
- Wow, whoo!
- ( applauding )

1434
00:54:46,980 --> 00:54:48,480
Here he comes.

1435
00:54:48,480 --> 00:54:49,980
( humming theme to
"The Dick Van Dyke Show" )

1436
00:54:52,990 --> 00:54:54,990
All right, shoot.

1437
00:54:55,490 --> 00:54:57,490
But first,
a few bird calls.

1438
00:54:57,490 --> 00:54:58,990
I have a brief statement.

1439
00:54:58,990 --> 00:55:02,500
The launch
of the XRV rescue craft

1440
00:55:02,490 --> 00:55:08,490
was scrubbed
at 8:47 Cape time.

1441
00:55:08,500 --> 00:55:11,510
The pad was shut down
at T-minus 58 seconds.

1442
00:55:11,500 --> 00:55:13,500
Last call was at 1:45.

1443
00:55:13,510 --> 00:55:15,510
When the wind velocity
in the launch area
rose to 48 knots.

1444
00:55:15,510 --> 00:55:18,010
And tiny sandwiches
are being put back
in deep freeze.

1445
00:55:18,010 --> 00:55:20,010
Commander Pruett in
the Apollo Command Module

1446
00:55:20,010 --> 00:55:21,520
has been informed.

1447
00:55:21,510 --> 00:55:26,010
He wept openly
like a little baby.

1448
00:55:26,020 --> 00:55:27,520
This...

1449
00:55:27,520 --> 00:55:30,020
Is this.

1450
00:55:30,020 --> 00:55:36,030
This was a very fine,
fine effort.

1451
00:55:36,030 --> 00:55:38,530
- Looks good.
- Do you have any questions?

1452
00:55:38,530 --> 00:55:40,030
Mr. Keith,
you say that--

1453
00:55:40,030 --> 00:55:42,040
Is there any way
I can look any dorkier?

1454
00:55:42,030 --> 00:55:44,530
Does that mean you're
going to reschedule
at a later time?

1455
00:55:44,540 --> 00:55:46,040
No, we have no plans.

1456
00:55:46,040 --> 00:55:47,040
Will there be any
future rescue attempts?

1457
00:55:47,040 --> 00:55:49,040
We have no plans.

1458
00:55:49,040 --> 00:55:50,550
Oh, no, that guy
was in Rocket Club,
definitely.

1459
00:55:50,540 --> 00:55:52,040
Mr. Keith, you say
the men have been informed.

1460
00:55:52,040 --> 00:55:53,040
Who informed them?

1461
00:55:53,050 --> 00:55:54,550
I did.

1462
00:55:54,550 --> 00:55:56,050
What was their reaction?

1463
00:55:56,050 --> 00:55:58,050
They liked it,
liked it a lot.

1464
00:55:59,550 --> 00:56:01,060
Do you have
any other questions?

1465
00:56:01,050 --> 00:56:04,050
I have a quote
from Mr. Hackman.
He said, "Aah!"

1466
00:56:04,060 --> 00:56:05,560
There's a general
assumption

1467
00:56:05,560 --> 00:56:08,560
that the space program
will be brought
to a halt

1468
00:56:08,560 --> 00:56:10,060
for an investigation
of the accident.

1469
00:56:10,060 --> 00:56:11,570
Check out Pee-Wee's
evil brother on the right.

1470
00:56:11,560 --> 00:56:12,560
Plus a general
examination

1471
00:56:12,560 --> 00:56:15,560
of our purposes in space
and the morality

1472
00:56:15,570 --> 00:56:18,070
of putting men
into space without
adequate--

1473
00:56:18,070 --> 00:56:19,070
What's your question?

1474
00:56:19,070 --> 00:56:20,580
Why is Richard Speck

1475
00:56:20,570 --> 00:56:22,070
looking over my shoulder
is my question?

1476
00:56:22,070 --> 00:56:23,570
Are the results you gained
worth the lives you lost?

1477
00:56:23,580 --> 00:56:25,080
You're damn right they are!

1478
00:56:25,080 --> 00:56:26,580
You wanna know what
they accomplished

1479
00:56:26,580 --> 00:56:28,580
living up there in
a tin can for five months?

1480
00:56:28,580 --> 00:56:30,590
Well, lots of stuff.

1481
00:56:30,580 --> 00:56:32,080
Because of men like these,

1482
00:56:32,080 --> 00:56:34,580
we've taken the first step
off this little planet.

1483
00:56:34,590 --> 00:56:36,090
The trip to the moon

1484
00:56:36,590 --> 00:56:37,590
was just a walk
around the block.

1485
00:56:37,590 --> 00:56:38,590
We're going to the stars.

1486
00:56:38,590 --> 00:56:40,090
Your little
small town pissant.

1487
00:56:40,090 --> 00:56:41,600
To other worlds,
other civilizations.

1488
00:56:41,590 --> 00:56:44,090
Mr. Keith?

1489
00:56:44,100 --> 00:56:45,100
I want this man
savagely beaten.

1490
00:56:45,100 --> 00:56:46,600
Men will be killed
in this effort,

1491
00:56:46,600 --> 00:56:48,600
just as they're killed
in cars and airplanes,

1492
00:56:48,600 --> 00:56:51,110
in bars and in bed.
What is it?

1493
00:56:51,100 --> 00:56:52,600
Are you sure you want
to say that?

1494
00:56:52,600 --> 00:56:53,600
- Look at the numbers.
- What about them?

1495
00:56:53,610 --> 00:56:54,610
The numbers, Mr. Keith,
the numbers.

1496
00:56:54,610 --> 00:56:56,110
What numbers?
Let me see.

1497
00:56:59,110 --> 00:57:00,620
In God's name.

1498
00:57:00,610 --> 00:57:03,610
I just won lotto.
I quit! Whoo!

1499
00:57:03,620 --> 00:57:05,620
( all shouting )

1500
00:57:05,620 --> 00:57:08,120
Well, these are some pics
that my kids drew.

1501
00:57:08,120 --> 00:57:11,630
Yeah, I hope
you like them.

1502
00:57:11,620 --> 00:57:14,120
Who cut out the ad?
I haven't even read
this yet.

1503
00:57:14,630 --> 00:57:15,630
We have 90-knot winds.

1504
00:57:15,630 --> 00:57:17,130
In the center or eye,
we have zero.

1505
00:57:17,130 --> 00:57:19,130
Oh, oh, Mr. Liddy?
Mr. Liddy?

1506
00:57:19,130 --> 00:57:20,130
It's over the Cape.

1507
00:57:20,130 --> 00:57:22,640
All I wish to hear
from each of you

1508
00:57:22,630 --> 00:57:25,130
is yes or no, go or no go.

1509
00:57:25,140 --> 00:57:26,640
Uh, go.

1510
00:57:26,640 --> 00:57:27,640
We have a launch window

1511
00:57:27,640 --> 00:57:28,640
while the eye
is over the Cape.

1512
00:57:28,640 --> 00:57:31,650
We affirm a launch window
at 22:31:06.

1513
00:57:31,640 --> 00:57:32,640
Can you launch your rocket

1514
00:57:32,640 --> 00:57:33,640
through the eye
of the hurricane?

1515
00:57:33,650 --> 00:57:34,650
- Yes, sir.
- If I felt like it.

1516
00:57:34,650 --> 00:57:36,150
Will the bird fly?

1517
00:57:36,150 --> 00:57:38,150
Well, it's been kicked
around by 80-knot winds.

1518
00:57:38,150 --> 00:57:40,150
- The data's a little--
- What's your judgment?

1519
00:57:40,150 --> 00:57:41,660
It'll fly.

1520
00:57:41,650 --> 00:57:42,650
- Carp?
- Yes, sir.

1521
00:57:42,650 --> 00:57:43,650
Carp?

1522
00:57:43,660 --> 00:57:45,160
Do we have 16 minutes
in the eye?

1523
00:57:45,160 --> 00:57:46,660
No, sir, not 16.

1524
00:57:46,660 --> 00:57:47,660
Do we have 14?

1525
00:57:47,660 --> 00:57:49,160
Well, we've been trying
to get a prog chart--

1526
00:57:49,160 --> 00:57:50,670
Come on, Carp,
yes or no!

1527
00:57:50,660 --> 00:57:51,660
What's your judgment?

1528
00:57:51,660 --> 00:57:53,160
I'll buy 14.

1529
00:57:53,170 --> 00:57:56,170
Can we move the tower
back in 12 minutes?

1530
00:57:56,170 --> 00:57:58,670
You give the order
and we'll do it.

1531
00:57:58,670 --> 00:58:00,170
Houston, Flight.

1532
00:58:00,170 --> 00:58:01,680
That's your night
retainer, sir.

1533
00:58:01,670 --> 00:58:03,670
Is the rescue craft
go for Port Charlie?

1534
00:58:03,680 --> 00:58:05,680
We can have
Point Charlie, yes,
but from there on in,

1535
00:58:05,680 --> 00:58:06,680
the pilot
will be on his own.

1536
00:58:06,680 --> 00:58:09,180
Rescue, can you rendezvous

1537
00:58:09,180 --> 00:58:10,690
without an onboard
computer program?

1538
00:58:10,680 --> 00:58:12,680
You just get me
in the ballpark.

1539
00:58:12,680 --> 00:58:14,180
As long as it's not
in the Metrodome.

1540
00:58:14,190 --> 00:58:18,690
Then we are go for launch
at 22:31:06 local time.

1541
00:58:18,690 --> 00:58:20,190
Just deal with it,
Carp.

1542
00:58:20,190 --> 00:58:23,200
22:31:06 local time.

1543
00:58:23,200 --> 00:58:27,700
Roger. 2-2-3-1-0-6
local time.

1544
00:58:27,700 --> 00:58:28,700
Give or take an hour.

1545
00:58:28,700 --> 00:58:30,200
We'll recycle
to T-minus 30.

1546
00:58:30,200 --> 00:58:32,710
That's Peter
Ueberroth there.

1547
00:58:34,210 --> 00:58:37,210
When this is over, let's
find a quiet spot, huh?

1548
00:58:37,210 --> 00:58:39,710
By 22:31:06,
the crew will be dead.

1549
00:58:39,710 --> 00:58:41,220
There's not enough
oxygen left

1550
00:58:41,210 --> 00:58:43,210
for three men
to live that long.

1551
00:58:43,220 --> 00:58:46,220
What about two men?

1552
00:58:46,220 --> 00:58:48,720
What about
six midgets?

1553
00:58:48,720 --> 00:58:50,220
We plot total pressure
against total use.

1554
00:58:50,220 --> 00:58:52,730
Is there sufficient oxygen
for two men?

1555
00:58:52,720 --> 00:58:55,220
What about two men
standing on each
others shoulders?

1556
00:58:55,230 --> 00:58:56,730
Or one?

1557
00:58:56,730 --> 00:58:59,230
How about two small
dogs and a bat?

1558
00:58:59,230 --> 00:59:00,740
A porcupine, perhaps?

1559
00:59:00,730 --> 00:59:02,230
Two might just make it.

1560
00:59:02,230 --> 00:59:04,730
Two small dogs
it is then.

1561
00:59:04,740 --> 00:59:08,740
Hey, someone left
the Cape out in the rain.

1562
00:59:08,740 --> 00:59:10,240
- Oh, yeah.
- I had to do it.

1563
00:59:11,740 --> 00:59:14,240
I do love
The Weather Channel.

1564
00:59:14,250 --> 00:59:16,750
It's good.
It really is good.

1565
00:59:16,750 --> 00:59:19,250
Now for this word
from Dial. ( laughs )

1566
00:59:19,250 --> 00:59:20,760
- I did it again.
- Stop it, you.

1567
00:59:20,750 --> 00:59:21,750
Sorry.

1568
00:59:21,750 --> 00:59:24,750
Gee, just trying to get
the BB in the bear's eye.

1569
00:59:24,760 --> 00:59:26,760
I love these
pocket puzzles.

1570
00:59:26,760 --> 00:59:28,260
Almost, yeah.

1571
00:59:28,260 --> 00:59:30,770
- Oh!
- Move back service tower.

1572
00:59:30,760 --> 00:59:33,760
( wind blows )

1573
00:59:36,770 --> 00:59:38,770
♪ We're out of the dark,
we're out of the woods ♪

1574
00:59:38,770 --> 00:59:40,780
♪ We're out of the night ♪

1575
00:59:40,770 --> 00:59:42,770
This is launch control.

1576
00:59:42,770 --> 00:59:45,270
We have just confirmed
that all spacecraft
systems are go

1577
00:59:45,280 --> 00:59:46,280
at this time.

1578
00:59:46,280 --> 00:59:47,780
Launch director
is about to feed

1579
00:59:47,780 --> 00:59:49,780
the final computer update
to the spacecraft.

1580
00:59:49,780 --> 00:59:51,790
Wow, check out all
the night crawlers!

1581
00:59:51,780 --> 00:59:53,780
This is
an unbelievable scene.

1582
00:59:53,790 --> 00:59:56,790
No wind.
Absolute silence.

1583
00:59:56,790 --> 01:00:00,800
Overhead, nothing but black
night and brilliant stars.

1584
01:00:00,790 --> 01:00:03,290
He's talking into
a Fudgsicle.

1585
01:00:03,300 --> 01:00:05,300
As the eye
of the Hurricane Alma
passes directly over us.

1586
01:00:05,300 --> 01:00:06,800
You say the magic word
and you get a $100.

1587
01:00:07,300 --> 01:00:08,300
There's an eerie hush
over everything here.

1588
01:00:08,800 --> 01:00:09,800
♪ All over the world ♪

1589
01:00:09,800 --> 01:00:11,810
No rain, no wind.

1590
01:00:11,800 --> 01:00:13,300
The lives of three men

1591
01:00:13,310 --> 01:00:16,310
are measured by
their fading heartbeats

1592
01:00:16,310 --> 01:00:19,810
and here on pad 41
at Cape Kennedy,

1593
01:00:19,810 --> 01:00:21,320
we're right in the middle
of another heartbeat.

1594
01:00:21,310 --> 01:00:23,810
It's a single moment
in the life of a great storm.

1595
01:00:23,820 --> 01:00:25,820
Vinyl seat covers
on the cameras.
How tacky.

1596
01:00:25,820 --> 01:00:28,320
A reprieve, we hope,
for Pruett, Lloyd and Stone.

1597
01:00:29,820 --> 01:00:32,330
The service tower is rolling
away from the launch pad.

1598
01:00:32,320 --> 01:00:33,820
( gasps )
Somebody stop it!

1599
01:00:33,830 --> 01:00:34,830
As soon as
it's 800 feet away,

1600
01:00:34,830 --> 01:00:35,830
it'll be locked
into position,

1601
01:00:35,830 --> 01:00:37,830
but they'll launch
before that happens.

1602
01:00:37,830 --> 01:00:39,830
That tower is expendable,

1603
01:00:39,830 --> 01:00:41,840
but the three men--

1604
01:00:42,330 --> 01:00:45,330
Are slightly less
expendable than
that tower.

1605
01:00:53,180 --> 01:00:56,680
Ironman One, Houston CapCom,
do you read?

1606
01:00:58,180 --> 01:01:01,180
Ironman One, Houston CapCom,
do you read?

1607
01:01:01,190 --> 01:01:04,690
Hey, isn't the machine
supposed to kick in
after four rings?

1608
01:01:04,690 --> 01:01:07,200
Ironman One, this is Houston
Flight, do you read me?

1609
01:01:07,190 --> 01:01:08,690
You want to trade seats
or something?

1610
01:01:08,690 --> 01:01:12,190
Ironman, this is Keith.

1611
01:01:12,200 --> 01:01:13,700
What the hell
do they want?

1612
01:01:13,700 --> 01:01:16,200
Flint. Flint!

1613
01:01:16,200 --> 01:01:18,710
Ugh, I'm up, I'm up.
What, what?

1614
01:01:18,700 --> 01:01:19,700
Hello, Jim, I want
to talk to you.

1615
01:01:19,710 --> 01:01:21,710
Yeah, what do you want?

1616
01:01:21,710 --> 01:01:23,210
I have some
numbers for you.

1617
01:01:23,210 --> 01:01:28,220
The eye of the hurricane
passed over the Cape
at 22:18,

1618
01:01:28,210 --> 01:01:33,210
and we had a second window
at 22:31:06 Cape time.

1619
01:01:33,220 --> 01:01:34,720
Now, I want you to add
those numbers.

1620
01:01:34,720 --> 01:01:36,220
I want to check out
the John Ritter
learning system.

1621
01:01:36,220 --> 01:01:37,230
We've successfully
launched the rescue craft

1622
01:01:37,220 --> 01:01:39,220
through the eye,
do you understand?

1623
01:01:41,730 --> 01:01:43,730
You have launched
the XRV?

1624
01:01:43,730 --> 01:01:46,730
Yes, that's correct.
The XRV has completed staging.

1625
01:01:46,730 --> 01:01:48,740
It's a Honda,
actually, a CRX.

1626
01:01:48,730 --> 01:01:51,230
It's only a two-seater,
so one of you has to go.

1627
01:01:51,240 --> 01:01:52,740
Our transfer
will be at 23:28.

1628
01:01:52,740 --> 01:01:55,740
That's 55 minutes
from now. Do you read?

1629
01:01:55,740 --> 01:01:58,750
Right, right. We'll
take a vote on it,

1630
01:01:58,740 --> 01:02:00,240
but we're kind of getting
used to the idea of death.

1631
01:02:00,750 --> 01:02:02,250
I told Stoney I loved him.

1632
01:02:02,250 --> 01:02:05,250
Jim, how do you, uh...

1633
01:02:05,250 --> 01:02:08,760
How do you evaluate
the oxygen situation?

1634
01:02:08,750 --> 01:02:12,750
What oxygen? ( laughs )
I kid. I'm just a cut-up.

1635
01:02:12,760 --> 01:02:14,760
Well, we have
whatever oxygen is left
in the spacecraft system.

1636
01:02:14,760 --> 01:02:17,270
And then there's
whatever's in the air.

1637
01:02:17,260 --> 01:02:20,260
Two bottles
of emergency oxygen on
board, five minutes each.

1638
01:02:20,270 --> 01:02:22,770
That's 10 man-minutes.

1639
01:02:22,770 --> 01:02:24,770
We can always squeeze
Stoner for a quart.

1640
01:02:24,770 --> 01:02:26,770
My backpack and Lloyd's
and Stone's...

1641
01:02:26,770 --> 01:02:29,280
Burp a little.

1642
01:02:29,270 --> 01:02:32,270
But there's not much
oxygen left in them.

1643
01:02:32,280 --> 01:02:35,280
Well, you'll have to
save your backpacks
for the EVA transfer.

1644
01:02:35,280 --> 01:02:36,780
- Yeah, I know that.
- Duh.

1645
01:02:37,280 --> 01:02:40,280
55 minutes.

1646
01:02:43,290 --> 01:02:45,790
Stoney's shrooming.
Look at him.

1647
01:02:45,790 --> 01:02:47,300
We'll be dead by then.

1648
01:02:47,290 --> 01:02:49,290
Well, that's how
I see it, too.

1649
01:02:49,290 --> 01:02:51,290
Looks like we solved
this problem. Thanks.

1650
01:02:51,300 --> 01:02:53,800
Continue to use oxygen
at the present rate.

1651
01:02:53,800 --> 01:02:56,800
Well, we can't cut down.

1652
01:02:56,800 --> 01:02:59,310
Let's think about that.

1653
01:02:59,300 --> 01:03:01,300
Won't you?
Thank you.

1654
01:03:01,310 --> 01:03:04,810
You want us to lower the
partial pressure again?

1655
01:03:05,310 --> 01:03:07,820
No, we've examined that.
It won't work.

1656
01:03:07,810 --> 01:03:10,310
Should we all beat around
the bush a little more?

1657
01:03:10,320 --> 01:03:12,820
Well, we're lying here
like corpses now.

1658
01:03:12,820 --> 01:03:15,820
What else can we do?

1659
01:03:15,820 --> 01:03:18,330
I'm glad
you brought that up.
Now you've got it.

1660
01:03:18,820 --> 01:03:19,820
You must think.

1661
01:03:19,830 --> 01:03:22,330
Duh, one, duh, two.

1662
01:03:25,830 --> 01:03:27,840
The guy in the middle,
he's the trouble-maker.
You know it.

1663
01:03:27,830 --> 01:03:31,330
Yeah,
we're thinking.

1664
01:03:31,340 --> 01:03:33,840
L-tay, only-stay,
m-scray.

1665
01:03:33,840 --> 01:03:36,340
Are we talking about
the same thing?

1666
01:03:36,340 --> 01:03:38,850
Are you talking to me?

1667
01:03:38,840 --> 01:03:40,340
Yeah.

1668
01:03:41,850 --> 01:03:44,350
Why don't you
talk it over?

1669
01:03:44,350 --> 01:03:46,350
If you could
work out something,

1670
01:03:46,350 --> 01:03:47,860
it would be
a great help.

1671
01:03:47,850 --> 01:03:49,850
Yeah, we'll
talk it over.

1672
01:03:49,860 --> 01:03:51,360
I must point out, Jim,

1673
01:03:51,360 --> 01:03:53,860
that any effective action
must be taken immediately.

1674
01:03:53,860 --> 01:03:55,360
Look, don't tell me
what to do!

1675
01:03:55,360 --> 01:03:57,870
We've been taking your
goddamn orders

1676
01:03:57,860 --> 01:03:58,860
and where the hell
are we?

1677
01:03:58,860 --> 01:04:00,860
From now on, we're gonna
make all the decisions!

1678
01:04:00,870 --> 01:04:02,870
Dirty anarchist.

1679
01:04:02,870 --> 01:04:04,370
Whatever we do,
you're out of it!

1680
01:04:04,370 --> 01:04:06,870
Oh, I appreciate
what you're saying, Jim,

1681
01:04:07,370 --> 01:04:08,870
and I agree with you.

1682
01:04:09,370 --> 01:04:09,870
You're exactly right.

1683
01:04:10,380 --> 01:04:11,380
You are correct, sir.

1684
01:04:11,380 --> 01:04:13,380
( clears throat )
Yeah, anything else?

1685
01:04:13,380 --> 01:04:15,380
Yes, to two of you,
good luck.

1686
01:04:15,380 --> 01:04:16,880
To one of you,
good-bye.

1687
01:04:16,880 --> 01:04:21,390
Would you please give me

1688
01:04:21,390 --> 01:04:22,390
those launch numbers
again?

1689
01:04:22,390 --> 01:04:27,400
Yes, that's launch
at 22:31:06 Cape time.

1690
01:04:27,390 --> 01:04:30,890
Rendezvous
and transfer at 23:28.

1691
01:04:30,900 --> 01:04:32,900
And breakfast
any ol' time.

1692
01:04:32,900 --> 01:04:34,400
Thanks.

1693
01:04:34,400 --> 01:04:36,400
For nothing.

1694
01:04:37,900 --> 01:04:39,900
( laughs ) Wait till they
find out I'm joking.

1695
01:04:39,910 --> 01:04:42,910
There's plenty of air
for everyone. ( laughs )

1696
01:04:42,910 --> 01:04:46,910
You know, they say someday
these will be cordless.

1697
01:04:48,910 --> 01:04:55,410
Launch at 22:31:06.
Rendezvous 23:28.

1698
01:04:55,420 --> 01:04:58,430
He's a pretty funny guy,
isn't he?

1699
01:04:58,420 --> 01:04:59,920
55 minutes to go.

1700
01:04:59,930 --> 01:05:00,930
Counting commercials?

1701
01:05:00,930 --> 01:05:03,430
Through the eye
of a hurricane.

1702
01:05:03,430 --> 01:05:04,430
Quit breathing so hard.

1703
01:05:04,430 --> 01:05:05,930
Jesus, 55 minutes.

1704
01:05:05,930 --> 01:05:07,440
Well, Stoney, you still
have that pillow?

1705
01:05:07,430 --> 01:05:11,430
Right. It'll be
pretty cold by then.

1706
01:05:11,440 --> 01:05:12,940
You know, when you put
your mouth down here,

1707
01:05:12,940 --> 01:05:13,940
you can make a funny noise.

1708
01:05:17,440 --> 01:05:22,440
Well, one of us
is gonna have to go.

1709
01:05:25,450 --> 01:05:26,450
I mean, uh...

1710
01:05:26,450 --> 01:05:28,460
I just went.

1711
01:05:28,450 --> 01:05:30,450
That's what we're talking
about, isn't it?

1712
01:05:30,460 --> 01:05:34,460
One of us goes
and the other two stay?

1713
01:05:34,460 --> 01:05:37,970
Well, I think it should be
old Zeppelin lungs Hackman.

1714
01:05:37,960 --> 01:05:40,460
I'll stay.
I want to be the one.

1715
01:05:40,470 --> 01:05:41,970
You're closest
to the door.

1716
01:05:41,970 --> 01:05:43,470
- I could move.
- What are we gonna do?

1717
01:05:44,970 --> 01:05:47,480
♪ Should I stay
or should I go? ♪

1718
01:05:47,470 --> 01:05:48,470
All right, let's
do it scientifically.

1719
01:05:48,470 --> 01:05:50,470
Two big guys throw
the little guy out, okay?

1720
01:05:50,480 --> 01:05:53,480
- ( all laugh )
- That's a good idea.

1721
01:05:56,480 --> 01:05:57,990
Um, well--

1722
01:05:57,980 --> 01:06:02,480
I wasn't joking, Gene.
Serves you right
for doing "Gypsy Moths."

1723
01:06:02,490 --> 01:06:03,990
Well, we're gonna have
to talk about this.

1724
01:06:03,990 --> 01:06:05,490
So what are we gonna do?

1725
01:06:05,990 --> 01:06:08,000
We already told you.
Throw you out.

1726
01:06:07,990 --> 01:06:09,490
Think we're joking?
Are we amusing you?

1727
01:06:09,490 --> 01:06:10,490
Do you think
we're clowns?

1728
01:06:10,500 --> 01:06:12,000
I could do it by chance.

1729
01:06:14,500 --> 01:06:16,500
I'll tear out
strips from a pad.

1730
01:06:16,500 --> 01:06:18,010
One short, two long.

1731
01:06:18,000 --> 01:06:19,500
The guy
with the short end goes.

1732
01:06:22,010 --> 01:06:23,010
Well, they could
flip a coin,

1733
01:06:23,510 --> 01:06:24,510
but it would never
come down.

1734
01:06:24,510 --> 01:06:26,010
They could play Yahtzee.

1735
01:06:26,010 --> 01:06:27,520
Play Yahtzee. Yeah.

1736
01:06:29,510 --> 01:06:31,010
Tear the little guy
in half.

1737
01:06:31,020 --> 01:06:33,020
Well, look, I don't want
to just lay here and die.

1738
01:06:33,020 --> 01:06:35,520
I want to shake the dirt
of this crumby capsule

1739
01:06:35,520 --> 01:06:37,020
off my shoes
and see the world.

1740
01:06:37,020 --> 01:06:39,030
Two guys, three guys,
is that much difference?

1741
01:06:39,020 --> 01:06:41,020
I say double the pills.
Put ourselves out.

1742
01:06:41,030 --> 01:06:43,530
Wait, are there five
all of a sudden?
Did I miss something?

1743
01:06:43,530 --> 01:06:45,030
Even when there's
no pressure,

1744
01:06:45,030 --> 01:06:47,540
there's still oxygen
molecules floating around.

1745
01:06:47,530 --> 01:06:49,530
You live for a while

1746
01:06:49,530 --> 01:06:51,530
even when there's no
pressure in the gauges.

1747
01:06:51,540 --> 01:06:53,540
Yeah, my cousin
did it once.

1748
01:06:53,540 --> 01:06:56,040
For God's sake,
it's less than 55 minutes!

1749
01:06:56,040 --> 01:06:59,550
- You're dreaming, Stone.
- All right. Okay.

1750
01:07:04,550 --> 01:07:06,550
- I'll go.
- Good.

1751
01:07:06,550 --> 01:07:08,560
Wait, I'm locked in.
I can't. I'm sorry.

1752
01:07:08,550 --> 01:07:10,050
I'm the weakest. I'm
using up the most oxygen.

1753
01:07:10,060 --> 01:07:12,560
I'll go. It's all right.

1754
01:07:12,560 --> 01:07:15,060
All right, all right.

1755
01:07:17,060 --> 01:07:19,570
It really isn't what you call
a win-win situation, is it?

1756
01:07:19,560 --> 01:07:20,560
Uh-uh, uh-uh.

1757
01:07:20,570 --> 01:07:22,070
All right,
get your helmets on.

1758
01:07:24,570 --> 01:07:26,070
I'm going out
to fix the engine.

1759
01:07:26,070 --> 01:07:29,580
Are you kidding? You
can't fix that engine.

1760
01:07:29,570 --> 01:07:31,570
Oh, who died and made you
President of the capsule?

1761
01:07:35,080 --> 01:07:36,580
There.

1762
01:07:36,580 --> 01:07:38,090
I don't know why I let
them talk me out of it.

1763
01:07:38,080 --> 01:07:40,080
I should've done this
two days ago.

1764
01:07:43,590 --> 01:07:45,090
Come on,
get your helmets on.

1765
01:07:47,090 --> 01:07:50,600
Hey, look,
it's a big old '59 Caddy

1766
01:07:50,600 --> 01:07:52,100
with a carbuncle on the back.

1767
01:07:54,100 --> 01:07:57,100
Anyone seen
my Grecian formula?

1768
01:07:57,100 --> 01:07:59,110
I left it here,
a small bottle,
says "Greg."

1769
01:08:03,110 --> 01:08:07,620
I think I'll play
a little Super Mario,
if you don't mind.

1770
01:08:07,610 --> 01:08:10,610
Okay. Don't everyone
talk at once.

1771
01:08:14,120 --> 01:08:16,620
Well, now, that was
a riveting scene, wasn't it?

1772
01:08:16,620 --> 01:08:17,630
- ( Crow whistle )
- Yeah.

1773
01:08:19,120 --> 01:08:23,120
Breathe deep
the gathering gloom.

1774
01:08:23,130 --> 01:08:27,640
Richard, unh-unh.
Don't touch that.

1775
01:08:27,630 --> 01:08:30,130
Unh-unh, no.

1776
01:08:30,140 --> 01:08:31,640
Hey, that's
the yogurt machine.

1777
01:08:31,640 --> 01:08:33,140
Don't you dare
go out there.

1778
01:08:33,140 --> 01:08:36,140
I don't want you
out there.

1779
01:08:36,140 --> 01:08:38,150
Well, while
he's going out,
let's us go out, huh?

1780
01:08:38,140 --> 01:08:39,140
Mm, let's us go out.

1781
01:08:39,140 --> 01:08:40,640
- Take a cue.
- Well said.

1782
01:08:40,650 --> 01:08:42,650
Hey, there's lots
of oxygen out there.

1783
01:08:53,860 --> 01:08:56,360
Hey, did you guys ever think
about what might happen

1784
01:08:56,360 --> 01:08:58,370
if one of us would
have to leave the ship

1785
01:08:58,360 --> 01:09:00,360
in order to save
the others?

1786
01:09:00,370 --> 01:09:02,370
- Uh, nope.
- Not really, no.

1787
01:09:02,870 --> 01:09:04,370
Really?
Not in the least?

1788
01:09:04,370 --> 01:09:06,370
Can't say as I have,
Skipper.

1789
01:09:06,370 --> 01:09:08,380
Yeah, pretty cut and dried
if you think about it.

1790
01:09:08,370 --> 01:09:09,870
Oh, come on,
you guys are saying

1791
01:09:09,870 --> 01:09:11,870
that this film hasn't
touched you in the least?

1792
01:09:11,880 --> 01:09:15,380
Three men
marooned in space.

1793
01:09:15,380 --> 01:09:16,880
You know, they're
addressing a problem

1794
01:09:16,880 --> 01:09:19,390
that we someday
may have to face.

1795
01:09:19,380 --> 01:09:22,380
And, you know, who can put
a value on human life?

1796
01:09:22,390 --> 01:09:25,390
I mean, who can decide
who lives and who dies?

1797
01:09:25,390 --> 01:09:26,890
I do, remember?
I called dibs.

1798
01:09:26,890 --> 01:09:28,900
Joel, I hate to be
the shriveled carrot

1799
01:09:28,890 --> 01:09:30,390
under your
peach cobbler, but--

1800
01:09:30,400 --> 01:09:32,900
Yeah, Joel, there really
isn't anything to talk about.

1801
01:09:32,900 --> 01:09:34,400
- You're a human.
- You need oxygen to breathe.

1802
01:09:34,400 --> 01:09:35,900
We don't.
We're robots.

1803
01:09:35,900 --> 01:09:37,400
Exceptionally cute
robots, I think.

1804
01:09:37,400 --> 01:09:38,910
Yes, exactly.
Oh, and clever, too.

1805
01:09:38,900 --> 01:09:40,400
I was gonna say
irreverent and witty.

1806
01:09:40,410 --> 01:09:42,410
- Droll, yet insightful.
- Oh, hardly different.

1807
01:09:42,410 --> 01:09:43,910
I'm already alone,
aren't I?

1808
01:09:50,950 --> 01:09:52,450
He'll fix it.

1809
01:09:52,450 --> 01:09:54,960
Oh. Hey, guys.
You're a big help.

1810
01:09:54,950 --> 01:09:56,450
He'll fix it.

1811
01:09:56,450 --> 01:09:59,950
You son of a bitch.
You know what
he's doing up there.

1812
01:09:59,960 --> 01:10:01,460
He's smoking.

1813
01:10:05,960 --> 01:10:06,960
Buzz, don't!

1814
01:10:06,970 --> 01:10:08,470
He told me he was
out of cigarettes.

1815
01:10:08,470 --> 01:10:10,970
Jim! Get back in here.
Jim, don't do it!

1816
01:10:10,970 --> 01:10:12,970
Okay, now where's the hood
on this goofy thing?

1817
01:10:12,970 --> 01:10:14,980
You're going to rip
out the umbilicals.

1818
01:10:14,970 --> 01:10:17,970
Jim, don't do it! Jim!

1819
01:10:17,980 --> 01:10:19,480
Jim!

1820
01:10:19,480 --> 01:10:20,980
I'm hiding down here.

1821
01:10:20,980 --> 01:10:23,980
Jim, don't do it! Jim!

1822
01:10:23,980 --> 01:10:26,990
Jim, come back in here
and put your mittens on!

1823
01:10:26,990 --> 01:10:29,490
No, Jim, don't.
Really, no, don't go.

1824
01:10:32,490 --> 01:10:37,000
Stoney? Stoney, give me
some line here. Stoney?

1825
01:10:37,000 --> 01:10:39,000
Feed me, Jack,
feed me!

1826
01:10:41,000 --> 01:10:44,000
Well, what do you know?
( laughs )

1827
01:10:44,000 --> 01:10:46,510
Jim, quick,
throw us your Rolex!

1828
01:10:46,500 --> 01:10:49,000
You know he's gonna
burst like a balloon.

1829
01:10:49,010 --> 01:10:50,010
It's sad really.

1830
01:10:50,010 --> 01:10:51,510
Mm. If you
think about it.

1831
01:10:52,010 --> 01:10:54,520
Doggone it, Jim,
you got the keys!

1832
01:10:58,020 --> 01:11:01,520
I'm melting...melting.

1833
01:11:01,520 --> 01:11:03,520
Oh, he cubed it, man.

1834
01:11:03,520 --> 01:11:06,530
I don't think
I can watch this. No way.

1835
01:11:06,520 --> 01:11:10,520
Dying is easy.
Comedy is hard.

1836
01:11:10,530 --> 01:11:12,530
Oh, so Richard Crenna
said that?

1837
01:11:12,530 --> 01:11:14,540
Yep, think so.

1838
01:11:14,530 --> 01:11:17,530
Either that matte
painting goes or I do.

1839
01:11:17,540 --> 01:11:19,540
Oh, he said that, too?

1840
01:11:19,540 --> 01:11:21,540
Wait, one last bit.

1841
01:11:21,540 --> 01:11:26,050
Like my Cagney?
You dirty rat...

1842
01:11:26,040 --> 01:11:28,040
Luke! Lukey, dagnabbit!

1843
01:11:28,050 --> 01:11:30,550
You get back
in this ship right now
before I tan your hide.

1844
01:11:30,550 --> 01:11:33,050
Hey, you didn't sign
our yearbook.

1845
01:11:39,560 --> 01:11:41,060
Hey, it's Matt Dillon
and Penny Marshall.

1846
01:11:43,060 --> 01:11:48,070
♪ I'd rather be a hammer
than a snail ♪

1847
01:11:48,070 --> 01:11:50,070
Huh? Oh.

1848
01:11:50,070 --> 01:11:52,070
Yeah, he's dead.

1849
01:11:52,070 --> 01:11:56,080
Can we still
have dinner?

1850
01:11:56,070 --> 01:11:57,070
Is that a no?

1851
01:11:57,080 --> 01:11:59,080
Well, you think
about it.

1852
01:11:59,080 --> 01:12:02,580
Tonight, the music
of Laserium.

1853
01:12:03,080 --> 01:12:06,590
Uh, Carl would
like to see you.

1854
01:12:09,090 --> 01:12:10,590
Haunting, isn't it?

1855
01:12:10,590 --> 01:12:13,090
Alan Sheppard in
a well-received cameo.

1856
01:12:17,600 --> 01:12:20,100
Oh, he must share an office
with Fred McMurray.

1857
01:12:25,100 --> 01:12:26,600
Major Healy here
will be right in.

1858
01:12:30,610 --> 01:12:32,110
Get out! Get out!

1859
01:12:32,110 --> 01:12:35,620
The calls are coming
from inside NASA!

1860
01:12:35,610 --> 01:12:38,110
If you have a husband
in space, press one.

1861
01:12:38,120 --> 01:12:40,120
If you have a dead
husband in space,
press two.

1862
01:12:40,120 --> 01:12:42,120
Mrs. Pruett, I wanted
to talk to you.

1863
01:12:42,120 --> 01:12:45,130
I'm calling from
your credit union.

1864
01:12:45,120 --> 01:12:46,620
Your husband is dead.

1865
01:12:49,130 --> 01:12:52,630
- And how are you?
- Way to let her down easy.

1866
01:12:52,630 --> 01:12:55,640
It was an accident.

1867
01:12:55,630 --> 01:12:58,630
We were playing
and, well, mommy said
I was too rough.

1868
01:12:58,640 --> 01:13:00,640
He was trying to repair
the spacecraft.

1869
01:13:00,640 --> 01:13:02,140
And he shot himself.

1870
01:13:04,640 --> 01:13:07,140
So I was wondering
if you'd like to have
some dinner.

1871
01:13:07,150 --> 01:13:08,650
You like Chinese?

1872
01:13:08,650 --> 01:13:12,150
I know this place.
It's called The Crash Pad.

1873
01:13:12,150 --> 01:13:14,150
So how are things
on your end?

1874
01:13:14,150 --> 01:13:16,660
Not so good, huh?
Well, my condolences.

1875
01:13:16,650 --> 01:13:18,650
I'm sending you
a case of steaks.

1876
01:13:18,660 --> 01:13:20,660
Hello? Really?

1877
01:13:20,660 --> 01:13:23,160
Please, let a smile
be your umbrella.

1878
01:13:23,160 --> 01:13:25,670
I speak from the--

1879
01:13:28,670 --> 01:13:31,670
Yay! It's not us!
Whoo! Whoo!

1880
01:13:31,670 --> 01:13:33,170
( whistles )

1881
01:13:33,170 --> 01:13:35,180
Now we got some room.
Thought he'd never leave.

1882
01:13:35,170 --> 01:13:37,670
Scooch over.

1883
01:13:37,680 --> 01:13:40,180
- Excuse me.
- I told you, Jim's not here.

1884
01:13:40,180 --> 01:13:41,680
He had an accident.

1885
01:13:41,680 --> 01:13:43,180
Dave's not here, man.

1886
01:13:43,180 --> 01:13:45,690
Where's Stoney at?

1887
01:13:45,680 --> 01:13:47,180
I'm here.

1888
01:13:49,690 --> 01:13:52,190
Stoney?

1889
01:13:52,190 --> 01:13:54,190
Dougherty's coming.

1890
01:13:54,190 --> 01:13:56,700
Man, they're really cutting
loose on that breathing thing

1891
01:13:56,690 --> 01:13:57,690
now that that guy's gone.

1892
01:13:57,700 --> 01:14:01,200
Yeah, they're breathing
for two now.

1893
01:14:01,200 --> 01:14:03,200
Stoney, tell Jim...

1894
01:14:03,200 --> 01:14:05,210
I love him.

1895
01:14:05,200 --> 01:14:06,200
To get us out of here.

1896
01:14:06,200 --> 01:14:10,200
Stop eating your oxygen,
damn you! Be still!

1897
01:14:10,210 --> 01:14:11,210
- It's Chuck Heston.
- Sounded like him.

1898
01:14:11,210 --> 01:14:15,220
Oh, look, the mothership.

1899
01:14:15,210 --> 01:14:16,710
The funkadelic
mothership?

1900
01:14:16,710 --> 01:14:18,210
- Range 76.8.
- Mm-hmm.

1901
01:14:18,220 --> 01:14:20,720
And since when did NASA
paint anything red?

1902
01:14:20,720 --> 01:14:22,220
I'm a little
under the line.

1903
01:14:22,220 --> 01:14:23,220
Rescue, Flight.

1904
01:14:23,220 --> 01:14:25,230
Janssen under the line?
Right.

1905
01:14:25,220 --> 01:14:28,220
Roger, attitude is go,
Delta V 63.

1906
01:14:28,230 --> 01:14:30,230
You know, either that ship
is tiny or he's huge.

1907
01:14:30,230 --> 01:14:33,730
Roger, 3, 2, 1.

1908
01:14:35,730 --> 01:14:36,730
Oh, that helped.
Watch this.

1909
01:14:37,240 --> 01:14:38,740
Oxygen.

1910
01:14:38,740 --> 01:14:41,240
- Can't.
- Nope.

1911
01:14:41,240 --> 01:14:42,740
Can't. Gotta save it
for the transfer.

1912
01:14:42,740 --> 01:14:45,750
No, not another bite
until we get to Grandma's.

1913
01:14:45,740 --> 01:14:48,740
I got a big scene coming up.
I need the oxygen.

1914
01:14:48,750 --> 01:14:50,250
Oh.

1915
01:14:50,250 --> 01:14:52,750
Oh, you're not dying.

1916
01:14:52,750 --> 01:14:54,760
I'm dying. Great.

1917
01:14:54,750 --> 01:14:57,750
It takes a long time to die.

1918
01:14:57,760 --> 01:14:59,760
Ironman, this is Rescue.

1919
01:15:01,260 --> 01:15:03,760
Ironman, this is Rescue.

1920
01:15:03,760 --> 01:15:05,770
Read you.

1921
01:15:05,760 --> 01:15:07,760
Read you? I love you.

1922
01:15:07,770 --> 01:15:09,770
Turn on your
acquisition lights.

1923
01:15:09,770 --> 01:15:11,770
Say again?

1924
01:15:12,270 --> 01:15:14,780
Come on, Stoney, turn on
your acquisition lights.

1925
01:15:15,270 --> 01:15:16,270
Turn on
your love light!

1926
01:15:16,770 --> 01:15:17,770
- Roger.
- And leave it on.

1927
01:15:17,780 --> 01:15:19,780
Roger. Acquisition lights.

1928
01:15:21,780 --> 01:15:24,280
Boy, are those bright.

1929
01:15:24,280 --> 01:15:25,790
Pretty bright lights.

1930
01:15:28,290 --> 01:15:30,290
Okay, Stoney, I'm coming
right down the pipe.

1931
01:15:30,290 --> 01:15:31,290
I'll see you
in six minutes.

1932
01:15:31,290 --> 01:15:33,790
Wow, you thought of me,
man. All right.

1933
01:15:33,790 --> 01:15:34,800
- Six minutes.
- Roger.

1934
01:15:34,790 --> 01:15:36,290
Buzz, six more minutes.

1935
01:15:36,290 --> 01:15:39,790
Phooey, phooey, phooey,
phooey, phooey on the ship.

1936
01:15:39,800 --> 01:15:41,300
Phooey on the ship
is what I say.

1937
01:15:41,300 --> 01:15:43,800
Can't...

1938
01:15:43,800 --> 01:15:45,810
You can, buddy.
You've got to!

1939
01:15:45,800 --> 01:15:48,800
Hey, everybody, it's
the Stoney and Buzz show.

1940
01:15:48,810 --> 01:15:51,310
Think of something nice.
Think, uh...

1941
01:15:51,310 --> 01:15:53,810
That Buzz has been
a raspberry seed in my
wisdom tooth long enough.

1942
01:15:53,810 --> 01:15:55,320
Think of the Earth, Buzz.

1943
01:15:56,810 --> 01:15:59,310
Look at the Earth, Buzz.

1944
01:15:59,320 --> 01:16:03,320
Look, can you tell
where we are?

1945
01:16:03,320 --> 01:16:04,830
Over the dells?

1946
01:16:04,820 --> 01:16:06,320
Stoney?

1947
01:16:06,320 --> 01:16:08,820
I'm right here, okay?

1948
01:16:08,830 --> 01:16:12,830
All right, Buzz.

1949
01:16:12,830 --> 01:16:14,330
All right.

1950
01:16:14,330 --> 01:16:16,840
You can sit
next to the window.

1951
01:16:20,840 --> 01:16:22,340
All right.

1952
01:16:23,840 --> 01:16:26,350
Time for that Flowbee
haircut. Come on.

1953
01:16:26,340 --> 01:16:28,340
All of you.

1954
01:16:28,350 --> 01:16:31,850
Okay, open wide.
Here comes papa.

1955
01:16:31,850 --> 01:16:33,350
Oh.

1956
01:16:33,350 --> 01:16:36,360
All right, Buzz,
you're on my air now.

1957
01:16:36,350 --> 01:16:38,350
Oh...oh, that's good.

1958
01:16:38,360 --> 01:16:40,360
Oh, my God,
what the heck are they
doing up there?

1959
01:16:40,360 --> 01:16:42,860
Cabin air. There
must be some left.

1960
01:16:51,870 --> 01:16:54,880
Hey, that's not half bad.

1961
01:16:54,870 --> 01:16:57,370
- Smells fresh.
- Hmm.

1962
01:17:02,810 --> 01:17:05,810
Oh, finally,
the cable guy's here.

1963
01:17:05,820 --> 01:17:07,320
- Took him long enough.
- The Russian cable guy.

1964
01:17:07,320 --> 01:17:08,820
Huh?

1965
01:17:10,820 --> 01:17:13,830
♪ In our Easter bonnets ♪

1966
01:17:13,820 --> 01:17:16,820
♪ With all the red,
black around it ♪

1967
01:17:20,830 --> 01:17:22,840
( laughs )

1968
01:17:22,830 --> 01:17:24,330
Who's laughing?

1969
01:17:24,340 --> 01:17:26,840
I can't figure it out.

1970
01:17:26,840 --> 01:17:29,340
Oh, the other Gene
is laughing.

1971
01:17:29,340 --> 01:17:33,350
Oh, Gene, I hooked you
into the nitrous.

1972
01:17:33,340 --> 01:17:36,840
- I'm sorry.
- ( laughs )

1973
01:17:36,850 --> 01:17:40,350
( rapid beeping )

1974
01:17:40,350 --> 01:17:42,860
Come here, you.
Tuck in that shirt.

1975
01:17:43,350 --> 01:17:45,350
You look ridiculous with
the tails hanging out.

1976
01:17:45,360 --> 01:17:47,360
( laughs )

1977
01:17:47,360 --> 01:17:50,860
That's enough
for you, dude.

1978
01:17:52,360 --> 01:17:54,360
Oh, roger. Dave, we
don't need you anymore.

1979
01:17:54,370 --> 01:17:56,870
The Russians rescued
them. Thanks anyway.

1980
01:17:56,870 --> 01:18:00,370
Roger, Ted, you've got
their acq lights.

1981
01:18:00,370 --> 01:18:02,380
( laughs )

1982
01:18:02,370 --> 01:18:05,370
Whoo-hoo, that's a good one.
That was number nine.

1983
01:18:05,380 --> 01:18:07,380
Whoo-hoo-hoo.

1984
01:18:07,380 --> 01:18:09,380
I kill me.

1985
01:18:09,380 --> 01:18:10,380
Blow the hatch.

1986
01:18:10,380 --> 01:18:14,390
Do not!
Do not blow the hatch!

1987
01:18:14,390 --> 01:18:17,890
( laughs ) Bite me.
I'm blowing the hatch.

1988
01:18:17,890 --> 01:18:22,400
Whoo-hoo. Oh, look.
We're heating the outside now.

1989
01:18:22,390 --> 01:18:25,390
Damn them!
Damn them all to hell!

1990
01:18:28,400 --> 01:18:30,400
( laughs )

1991
01:18:30,400 --> 01:18:33,910
All right, chuckles,
up you go. Come on.

1992
01:18:36,910 --> 01:18:41,920
♪ There's a bright
golden haze on the meadow ♪

1993
01:18:41,910 --> 01:18:44,410
